// Longer write-ups for spot pages, shown under "About this spot". Keyed by spot slug. The HTML follows the
// guide rules in guides.js, and [[slug]] or [[slug|text]] link to other spots (a missing spot fails the build).
// A spot listed in hidden-spots.js comes back into Google as soon as it has an entry here.
module.exports = {

'shinobazu-pond': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Go early. Lotus flowers open at dawn and start closing by late morning, so a visit between 7 and 9 am sees them at their best, while one after lunch mostly shows leaves and closed buds. The pond is part of Ueno Park, which opens at 5 am, so you can be there before the heat and the crowds. Peak is usually late July to early August, but some flowers keep opening from mid July to mid August.</p>
<p>Outside summer the pond is still worth a look. The lotus stems turn brown and fold over in autumn, a view Japanese photographers seek out in its own right, and the cherry trees along the path round the edge flower in late March and early April.</p>
<h3>Around the pond</h3>
<p>Shinobazu is split into three parts. The lotus grows in the southern section, the Lotus Pond. The Boat Pond to the northwest rents out rowing boats and swan pedal boats, and the third section, the Cormorant Pond, belongs to Ueno Zoo. Bentendo, an octagonal hall to the goddess Benzaiten, sits on a small island reached by a causeway, and it's free to walk in.</p>
<p>It's an easy add to a day in Ueno. [[ueno-park]] and its museums are just up the slope, and the [[ueno-toshogu-peony-garden|Toshogu peony garden]] is a five minute walk. Ameyoko market is a few minutes' walk from the southern end.</p>
`,

'ueno-toshogu-peony-garden': `
<h3>Why go in winter</h3>
<p>The winter peonies are the reason to come. Each plant stands under its own little straw hat, a <em>yuki-gakoi</em> shelter that keeps off frost and snow, and the flowers come out in the coldest weeks of the year, when almost nothing else in Tokyo is blooming. The winter garden usually opens on 1 January and runs until late February. It's small, so 30 to 45 minutes is plenty.</p>
<p>We'd go on a sunny weekday morning. The garden gets busy over the New Year holidays, when people combine it with their first shrine visit of the year, and the flowers look their best in low winter sun.</p>
<h3>The other seasons</h3>
<p>The garden opens again for a bigger peony show in spring, roughly mid April to early May, with around 500 plants. In autumn it now has a dahlia festival too, which ran from 19 September to 3 November in 2026.</p>
<h3>The shrine next door</h3>
<p>Ueno Toshogu enshrines Tokugawa Ieyasu, and its gold-covered main hall dates from 1651. It's worth the few extra minutes: the path in is lined with stone and bronze lanterns given by the lords of the Edo period. [[shinobazu-pond]] is a five minute walk downhill, and the rest of [[ueno-park]] is all around you.</p>
`,

'nakayama-ofuji': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Aim for the Nakayama Ofuji Festival, usually held over two weeks from mid to late April. In 2026 it ran from 15 to 28 April, and the dates move a little each year with the flowers. During the festival the trellis is lit up in the evening, and we think it's at its best in the hour after dark, when the tresses glow against the black sky.</p>
<p>Weekday afternoons are quietest. On weekends the small shrine grounds fill up quickly, and the narrow roads around it get congested.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The shrine is in the countryside north of central Yanagawa, so it's hard to reach without a car outside the festival. During the festival, temporary buses usually run from Nishitetsu-Yanagawa Station and JR Chikugo-Funagoya Station, and there are temporary car parks with a shuttle. Check Yanagawa City's tourism site before you go, because the bus timetable changes every year.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>Yanagawa itself is the canal town of Kyushu. A punted boat ride along its old moats takes about an hour, and the local dish is <em>unagi no seiromushi</em>, eel steamed over rice in a lidded box. If you'd like another wisteria the same day, the [[kuroki-great-wisteria]] in Yame, said to be about 600 years old, flowers at the same time of year.</p>
`,

'hachioji-koshu-kaido-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually turn fully yellow in the second half of November. The Hachioji Icho Festival is held on a weekend in mid November, with food stalls and events along the road. In 2026 it's on 21 and 22 November, and it marks 100 years since the avenue was planted. Entry is free, and there's no festival parking, so go by train.</p>
<p>The festival weekend is lively but crowded. For photos of the trees on their own, we'd pick a weekday in the last week of November, when the leaves are often at their peak and starting to carpet the pavement.</p>
<h3>How to walk it</h3>
<p>The avenue runs for about 4 km along the old Koshu Kaido highway, from the Oiwake junction in Hachioji to the Takao area. You don't need to walk all of it. Nishi-Hachioji Station is right on the avenue, so start there and walk for as long as you like. The trees were planted to honour the Taisho Emperor, whose tomb is at the Musashi Imperial Graveyard near Takao.</p>
<p>From the western end you're close to Takao, the base of [[mount-takao]], where the maples on the trail turn red at the same time. It makes a full autumn day out from central Tokyo, about an hour away on the Chuo Line.</p>
`,

'ueno-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Ueno usually reaches full bloom in the last week of March, a few days either side of Tokyo's official date, and the main avenue stays pink for about a week. The Ueno Cherry Blossom Festival runs through the season, with paper lanterns along the central path in the evening.</p>
<p>It's one of the busiest hanami spots in Tokyo. On a sunny weekend at full bloom, the picnic sheets go down soon after dawn and the central avenue moves at walking pace. If you want room to breathe, come on a weekday before 8 am, or walk the quieter path round [[shinobazu-pond]] at the bottom of the hill, where the trees hang over the water.</p>
<h3>What else is here</h3>
<p>Ueno Park isn't only for cherry blossoms. The Tokyo National Museum, the National Museum of Western Art and Ueno Zoo are all inside it, so a rainy day still works. The [[ueno-toshogu-peony-garden|Toshogu peony garden]] flowers in winter and again in spring.</p>
<p>The cherries at [[sumida-park]] in Asakusa are three stops away on the Ginza Line, and [[nezu-shrine]] is about 20 minutes on foot through the old streets of Yanaka, which makes a good walk if you're here in April for the azaleas.</p>
`,

'ruriko-in-temple': `
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>Ruriko-in only opens for special viewings in spring and autumn, so check the dates before anything else. The 2026 autumn opening runs from 1 October to 13 December. Entry has been 2,000 yen in recent years, and during the busiest weeks of November visits have needed booking in advance, so look at the temple's official site well before your trip.</p>
<p>The maples usually peak in mid to late November, but the famous reflections work before peak too. Green leaves in October and early November reflect just as clearly, and the crowds are smaller.</p>
<h3>The reflections</h3>
<p>The view everyone comes for is upstairs in the study. The maples outside reflect in the polished black lacquer of a long desk, and in the floor, so the garden appears twice. At busy times you may have to wait your turn for the best spot. Downstairs, the Ruri no Niwa garden is a moss garden with a stream running through it.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take the Eizan Railway from Demachiyanagi to Yase-Hieizanguchi, the end of the line. The temple is a short walk across the river. Ohara and its temples are a bus ride further north, and the cable car up Mount Hiei leaves from near the station, so you can make a full day of the north of Kyoto. For more maples in the city, see [[eikando-temple]] and [[tofuku-ji-temple]].</p>
`,

'yoyogi-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak in the last week of March and the first days of April. Yoyogi is the most relaxed of the big hanami parks in central Tokyo: the lawns are huge, the crowd is young, and on spring weekends there's music, food and groups who've been there since morning. If you want a party atmosphere, it's our pick over the more formal parks.</p>
<p>On a sunny weekend at full bloom, the best spots under the trees go by mid morning. Come early with your sheet, or come on a weekday, when there's plenty of room.</p>
<h3>Other seasons</h3>
<p>The park changes colour again in late November, when its ginkgo trees turn yellow and the leaves cover the paths. In any season it's a good break from Harajuku, and it's open around the clock.</p>
<h3>Next door</h3>
<p>Meiji Jingu, Tokyo's biggest shrine, is right beside the park, and its [[meiji-jingu-inner-garden|inner garden]] has an iris field that flowers in June. In November the shrine holds its [[meiji-jingu-chrysanthemum-show|chrysanthemum show]]. For another big hanami park, [[inokashira-park]] in Kichijoji is about half an hour away by train and has rowing boats under the trees.</p>
`,

'nezu-shrine': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The azalea garden only opens for the Bunkyo Azalea Festival, which runs through April. Different kinds flower at different times, so the hillside is at its fullest from mid to late April. Entry is a donation of 500 to 1,000 yen, set by how far the flowers are out, and children of primary school age and under go free.</p>
<p>Weekends at peak are crowded, with queues on the narrow paths through the garden. We'd go on a weekday as soon as it opens at 9 am, when the light is soft and you can stop for photos.</p>
<h3>The shrine</h3>
<p>Nezu is worth a visit at any time of year. Its main buildings date from 1706 and are designated Important Cultural Properties. They survived the 1923 earthquake and the bombing of the war. Beside the azalea slope, a tunnel of small red torii gates leads to the Otome Inari shrine, a quieter version of the famous gates of Kyoto.</p>
<h3>Around the shrine</h3>
<p>Nezu sits in Yanesen, the old neighbourhoods of Yanaka, Nezu and Sendagi, with small shops, temples and cafes in streets that feel older than most of Tokyo. Walk south and [[ueno-park]] is about 20 minutes away. For more azaleas, [[rikugien-garden]] in Komagome is a short ride away.</p>
`,

'hitachi-seaside-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The nemophila usually come into full colour in mid to late April. In 2026 the park expected full bloom around 16 April. The flowers last for about two weeks, but the blue fades as they go over, so aim for the second half of April if you can, and avoid the Golden Week holidays from late April to early May, when the park and the roads around it are packed.</p>
<p>During the nemophila season the park opens early, at 7 am, on some of the busiest days. That's the best time to go: the hill is quieter and the morning light on the flowers is soft.</p>
<h3>Tickets</h3>
<p>In 2026, adult entry was 450 yen, plus a seasonal charge of 350 yen during the nemophila weeks, so 800 yen in total. Children of junior high school age and under go free. The park is normally closed on Tuesdays, but it opens every day in spring.</p>
<h3>Getting there and other seasons</h3>
<p>Take the JR Joban Line to Katsuta, then the bus to the park's west entrance. From Tokyo it's about two hours in total. Mito, one stop before Katsuta, has [[kairakuen-garden]], one of the best plum gardens in Japan. The park is worth a second trip in October, when the same hill turns red with [[hitachi-seaside-park-october|kochia]].</p>
`,

'kyu-furukawa-gardens': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The roses flower twice a year. The spring show, from mid to late May, is the bigger one, with more blooms and stronger scent. The autumn show from October to early November has fewer flowers but deeper colours, and the garden is quieter. The rose festivals are held at both times. Entry is 150 yen, among the cheapest garden tickets in Tokyo.</p>
<p>Weekday mornings are calmest. On spring weekends at peak, the terraces get busy and you'll wait for a clear shot of the mansion.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The house and the rose garden were designed by Josiah Conder, the British architect who built much of Meiji-era Tokyo. The stone mansion was finished in 1917. Below the rose terraces, the slope drops down to a Japanese garden with a pond, designed by the garden maker Ogawa Jihei. It's one of the few places in Tokyo where you can see Western and Japanese gardens side by side. Give it an hour.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>The garden is a 7 minute walk from Kami-Nakazato Station on the JR Keihin-Tohoku Line, or about 12 minutes from Komagome. From Komagome, [[rikugien-garden]], an Edo-period stroll garden known for its maples and weeping cherry, is a short walk the other way, so you can see both in an afternoon.</p>
`,

'hanamiyama-park': `
<h3>Why it's special</h3>
<p>Hanamiyama isn't a public park in the usual sense. It's a hillside of flower farms owned by the Abe family, who began opening it to visitors for free in 1959. The photographer Akiyama Shotaro called it a <em>togenkyo</em>, a hidden paradise, and the name stuck. What makes it different from a cherry park is the mix: cherries, forsythia, magnolia, quince and peach all flower in the same few weeks, so the whole hill is pink, yellow and white at once.</p>
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The best weeks are usually early to mid April. The somei-yoshino cherries peak around the first or second week of April, with other trees flowering just before and after. Weekdays are far calmer. On a sunny weekend at peak, the path up the hill can be slow.</p>
<p>Signposted walking courses go up the hill, roughly 30, 60 and 90 minutes long. The longer ones climb higher and give the best views over the farms towards the snowy Azuma mountains.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>During the flower season, traffic around the hill is restricted and a temporary bus, the Hanamiyama-go, runs from the east exit of Fukushima Station. From the bus stop it's about a 15 minute walk. If you drive, you park at a temporary car park outside the area and take a shuttle, which has come with a 500 yen fee per person. Fukushima is about 90 minutes from Tokyo by shinkansen, so it works as a long day trip.</p>
`,

'takada-castle-ruins-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Joetsu is on the snowy Sea of Japan side, so the cherries flower later than in Tokyo, usually in the first half of April. The Takada cherry festival runs through the season. In 2026 it was the 101st, held from 3 to 19 April.</p>
<p>Come after dark. About 3,000 paper lanterns, <em>bonbori</em>, are lit along the paths, and the rebuilt three-storey turret is lit up and reflected in the moat with the cherries around it. The light-up usually runs from about 6 pm to 9 pm, and later at peak. It's the reason Takada is counted among Japan's three great night-cherry spots, and we'd plan the trip around an evening here.</p>
<h3>In the park</h3>
<p>The park itself is free. The turret has a small museum inside, about 310 yen for adults, with a view over the castle grounds. Takada is also known in summer, when lotus flowers fill the outer moat, one of the biggest lotus displays in Japan, from July to August.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take the Hokuriku Shinkansen to Joetsumyoko, then the Echigo Tokimeki Railway a few minutes north to Takada. The park is 15 minutes on foot from Takada Station, and a shuttle bus runs at peak. Book a hotel early if you're coming for the festival weekend.</p>
`,

'okayama-korakuen': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Korakuen is one of Japan's three great gardens, with [[kenrokuen]] in Kanazawa and [[kairakuen-garden]] in Mito, and it's worth seeing in any season. For lotus, go in July. On one early July morning each year, the Kanrensetsu, the garden opens at 4 am so you can watch the flowers open at dawn. In 2026 it was on 5 July. Later in summer, the garden opens at night for the Summer Fantasy Garden illumination, usually in August.</p>
<p>In spring, the garden's plum grove and cherry grove make it a good stop from February to early April, and the maples turn in November.</p>
<h3>Practical details</h3>
<p>Entry is 500 yen for adults, and high school students and younger go free. The garden opens at 7:30 am from late March to September and at 8 am the rest of the year, closing at 6 pm in summer and 5 pm in winter. The garden was finished in 1700 for the lord of Okayama, and its open lawns, tea houses and ponds take about an hour to walk round.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>From Okayama Station, take the tram to Shiroshita, then walk about 10 minutes across the river. Okayama Castle, the black "crow castle", stands right on the other bank, so you can see both in one morning.</p>
`,

'ritsurin-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Ritsurin is one of the finest daimyo gardens in Japan, with six ponds and 13 small hills set against Mount Shiun. The plum groves flower first, usually in February, with a plum festival early in the month. About 300 cherry trees follow from late March to early April.</p>
<p>For cherries, aim for the spring light-up. In 2026 it ran for about ten days around the end of March and start of April, from 6 pm to 9 pm, at the normal entry price. The garden opens at night again in late autumn for the maples, from 20 to 29 November in 2026.</p>
<h3>What to do there</h3>
<p>Give it at least 90 minutes. The garden's views were laid out to be seen on foot, and the most famous one, from Engetsu-kyo bridge over the South Pond, is worth walking to. You can stop for matcha at the Kikugetsu-tei tea house, which looks out over the water. During the light-ups, traditional boats take visitors round the North Pond. They need booking in advance.</p>
<p>Entry is 500 yen for adults and 170 yen for junior high school students and younger.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The garden is in Takamatsu, three minutes on foot from Ritsurin-koen Kitaguchi Station on the JR Kotoku Line, or a short walk from Ritsurin-koen Station on the Kotoden. Takamatsu is also where the ferries leave for the art islands of Naoshima and Teshima, so it's easy to fit in on a trip there.</p>
`,

'nabana-no-sato': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Most people come for the winter illumination, one of the biggest in Japan. The 2026 to 2027 season runs from 17 October 2026 to 31 May 2027, with the park closed on 6 and 7 January. The lights come on around sunset. The main display changes its theme every year, and the tunnel of light, about 200 m long, is the highlight we'd plan around.</p>
<p>Go on a weekday if you can, and arrive before dark so you see the lights come on. Weekends and the Christmas season are the busiest.</p>
<h3>Tickets</h3>
<p>The entry price changes through the season. For the first weeks of the 2026 illumination it was about 2,700 yen at the gate, rising to about 3,200 yen from late November. Tickets bought in advance at convenience stores are a little cheaper. Entry includes the Begonia Garden, a big greenhouse where begonias flower all year.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Nabana no Sato is on Nagashima, between Nagoya and Kuwana. Buses run from Nagashima Station, and during the illumination there are direct buses from the Meitetsu Bus Center in Nagoya. Parking is free and large, but the roads back towards Nagoya jam on busy evenings. Nagashima Spa Land and the Jazz Dream Nagashima outlet mall are next door, so it's easy to make a full day of it.</p>
`,

'byodo-in-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria usually flowers from mid April to the start of May. The trellis by the pond is at its best for about a week, when the clusters hang longest, and the temple cuts them back soon after the Golden Week holidays. Come at opening time on a weekday. Uji fills up quickly on spring weekends, and the best view of the wisteria and the Phoenix Hall together is from a small stretch of path.</p>
<h3>Seeing the Phoenix Hall</h3>
<p>The Phoenix Hall was built in 1053, and it's one of the oldest wooden buildings in the Kyoto area. Your entry ticket covers the garden and the Hoshokan museum, where the original bronze phoenixes from the roof and the carved figures of bodhisattvas on clouds are on show. Going inside the hall itself costs a little extra. Tickets for timed groups are sold in the garden from 9:10 am on the day, and on busy days they sell out early, so get one as soon as you arrive.</p>
<h3>Around Uji</h3>
<p>Uji is famous for green tea, and the street from the station to the temple is lined with tea shops selling matcha sweets and ice cream. Across the river, Ujigami Shrine is another World Heritage site, with a main hall from the 11th century. In June, [[mimuroto-ji-temple]] in the hills behind Uji has one of the best hydrangea gardens in Kansai. Uji is about 20 to 30 minutes from Kyoto Station on the JR Nara Line.</p>
`,

'hamarikyu-gardens': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The rapeseed field is usually at its best from late February to mid March, when its yellow flowers stand out against the glass towers of Shiodome. The plum trees flower around the same time or a little before, so a visit in late February often catches both. In spring the garden also has cherries, and in summer and autumn it's one of the calmest places to sit in central Tokyo.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Hamarikyu was a garden of the Tokugawa shoguns, and it was used for duck hunting. Its big pond is fed by seawater from Tokyo Bay, and the water level rises and falls with the tide. On the island in the pond, the Nakajima teahouse serves matcha and a Japanese sweet with a view over the water. We think it's one of the best-placed tea stops in the city. Entry to the garden is 300 yen.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Walk 7 minutes from Shiodome or Tsukijishijo Station, so a visit combines well with breakfast at Tsukiji Outer Market. Or arrive by water: a water bus runs between Asakusa and the garden's own pier, about 35 minutes along the Sumida River. Check whether your water bus ticket includes garden entry, as this has varied.</p>
`,

'odawara-castle-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Gyokan no Fuji wisteria usually flowers from mid to late April, with clusters more than a metre long. It's free to see and stands in the castle grounds below the keep. Come on a weekday morning, when it's quiet enough to take photos without a crowd under the trellis.</p>
<p>The castle has a second flower season in late March and early April, when about 300 cherry trees flower around the moats and the keep is lit up in the evening.</p>
<h3>The castle</h3>
<p>The keep was rebuilt in 1960 and is a museum inside, with a small entry fee and a view from the top over Sagami Bay. It was the seat of the Hojo clan, who held much of eastern Japan until 1590.</p>
<h3>Getting there and nearby</h3>
<p>The castle is 10 minutes on foot from Odawara Station, about 35 minutes from Tokyo by shinkansen or about 75 minutes from Shinjuku on the Odakyu Romancecar. There's no car park for the castle, so take the train. Odawara is also the gateway to Hakone. The [[hakone-tozan-railway]], famous for its hydrangeas in June, starts nearby, and in February the [[soga-plum-groves]] just outside the city are one of the biggest plum displays near Tokyo.</p>
`,

'awaji-island-national-government-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulips are usually at their best in early April. In 2025 the park planted about 168,000 tulips of 100 kinds, flowering from mid March to mid April. The beds slope down a hillside with Osaka Bay behind, so a sunny morning gives the best colour.</p>
<h3>Tickets</h3>
<p>There's a small entry fee for adults, and children of junior high school age and younger go free. Check the official site before you go, because the fee and any free days change from year to year.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>The park is right next to Awaji Yumebutai, a complex designed by the architect Tadao Ando, with the Hyakudanen, a hillside of 100 stepped square flower beds. It's free to walk round and well worth an hour. On the way, the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge, one of the longest suspension bridges in the world, takes you over to the island.</p>
<p>Highway buses from Sannomiya in Kobe stop at Awaji Yumebutai in about an hour. With a car, you could add [[awaji-hanasajiki]], a free hilltop flower park elsewhere in the north of the island.</p>
`,

'yokohama-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulips usually peak from early to mid April. About 100,000 tulips of some 70 kinds are planted in the beds around Yokohama Stadium, and they're part of the Garden Necklace Yokohama festival, which runs across the city in spring. It's free and open all day, and right next to Kannai Station, so it's one of the easiest flower stops in the Tokyo area.</p>
<p>Weekday mornings are best for photos. On weekends, and on evenings when the Yokohama DeNA BayStars are playing at home, the park gets busy.</p>
<h3>Walk to the waterfront</h3>
<p>Yokohama Park makes a good start to a walk through the city. Chinatown is about 5 minutes away. Head towards the sea along Nihon-odori, which also has tulip beds in spring, and you reach [[yamashita-park]], where the rose garden flowers in May. Up the hill, [[minato-no-mieru-oka-park]] has another rose garden and a view over the harbour. For a bigger garden, [[yokohama-english-garden]] is best known for its roses.</p>
`,

'shimizu-park-november': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually turn from late November into early December. In 2024 the Momiji-dani was at its best from about 29 November to 9 December, which is typical, so the first week of December is a safe bet. It's later than the mountains around Tokyo, because the park sits on the flat land of northern Chiba.</p>
<p>The Momiji-dani is free and has no opening hours, so you can walk it early, before the day-trippers arrive. Bring decent shoes: it's a path through a small valley, and the fallen leaves get slippery after rain.</p>
<h3>Around the park</h3>
<p>The park and its maple valley are free, and you only pay for extras such as the big field athletics course, which is popular with families. For more autumn colour, walk the approach from the Nio gate to the main hall of Kinjo-in, the temple in the park grounds. Parking is paid, so the train is easier: Shimizu-koen Station on the Tobu Urban Park Line is about 10 minutes' walk.</p>
<p>The same park has two other seasons on our calendar: its old [[shimizu-park|plum garden]] in February, and [[shimizu-park-april|azaleas and cherries]] in spring.</p>
`,

'kayumi-sasanqua': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree usually flowers from late November into early December, and the view people come for needs both trees at once: the sasanqua's pink in front and the ginkgo's yellow behind. That overlap only lasts a week or two, and in some years the sasanqua is late. In 2025 it was just opening on 24 November, with full bloom expected around 10 December. Check with the Iinan area office of Matsusaka City (0598-32-2511) before a long trip.</p>
<p>The tree stands about 11 m tall at the edge of a tea field, so the whole scene fits in one frame from the lane below. Please stay off the tea rows: this is working farmland.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>From Matsusaka Station, take a Mie Kotsu bus bound for Iitaka and get off at Kashiwano, about 50 minutes, then walk 5 minutes. Buses are infrequent, so plan your return before you set off. Iinan is a tea district known for deep-steamed sencha, and the Iinan tea heritage hall nearby explains how it's grown and made, though it's closed at weekends.</p>
<p>Matsusaka is also the home of Matsusaka beef, so a beef lunch back in town rounds off the day. In late February, the [[kasamatsu-kawazu-cherry-road]] is Matsusaka's early cherry spot.</p>
`,

'izu-shirahama-aloe-fields': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Go during the Aloe Flower Festival, which usually runs from late November into early January. The 2025 to 2026 festival ran from 30 November to 4 January, from 9 am to 3 pm. The red spikes are usually at their fullest in December. Entry is free, and the dates for the 2026 to 2027 season should be announced by the Shirahama tourist association in November.</p>
<p>Come on a sunny day. The aloe grows on a slope facing the Pacific, and the red flowers look best with blue sea behind them.</p>
<h3>At the festival</h3>
<p>The venue, the Izu Shirahama Aloe no Sato, has a stall selling aloe products and local food such as <em>sanma-zushi</em>, pressed mackerel pike sushi, and they usually serve aloe tea free. It's a quick stop, half an hour at most, so pair it with something else on the southern tip of Izu.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Shirahama Beach, a long stretch of white sand, is a few minutes along the coast, and Shimoda town is about 15 minutes by bus. In summer, [[shimoda-park]] is a hydrangea spot, and in February the [[minami-izu-cherry-and-rapeseed-festival]] and the [[kawazu-river]] cherries are a short drive away.</p>
`,

'arita-great-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree usually turns gold in the second half of November, and that's also when the town holds the Autumn Arita Ceramics Fair. In 2026 the fair runs from 19 to 23 November, with pottery sales and events around town, so the tree and the fair line up nicely. It gets busy then, so go early in the day.</p>
<p>The leaves drop fast once they're at their peak. By early December in 2025 more than half had fallen.</p>
<h3>Izumiyama</h3>
<p>The ginkgo stands at Izumiyama, a few minutes from the Izumiyama quarry. This is where porcelain stone was found in the early 1600s, which made Arita the birthplace of Japanese porcelain. The quarry is a huge bowl cut into the hill, and you can look down into it from the edge.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a 10 minute walk from Kami-Arita Station on the JR Sasebo Line. Drivers can use the Rokuroza car park, about 3 minutes' walk away. From there it's a pleasant downhill walk through the old merchant street of Uchiyama, lined with pottery shops, back towards the centre of town.</p>
`,

'nihon-odori-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn yellow in late November and stay that way into early December. The photo most people come for is the yellow trees against the old Kanagawa Prefectural Government building, whose tower locals call the King.</p>
<p>The avenue is only a few hundred metres long, so 20 minutes covers it. It's free, open all day, and right on top of Nihon-odori Station.</p>
<h3>A Yokohama ginkgo walk</h3>
<p>Nihon-odori is the middle of an easy autumn walk. Start at [[yokohama-park]], walk down Nihon-odori to Zou-no-Hana Park on the water, then turn right along the [[yamashita-koen-dori-ginkgo-avenue]], which runs beside [[yamashita-park]]. The whole loop is about 2 km and flat.</p>
<p>Chinatown is a few minutes away for lunch. In spring, the same street has tulip beds as part of the Garden Necklace Yokohama festival.</p>
`,

'bodai-ji-temple-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgo usually starts turning in mid November and is at its most yellow in the second half of the month, though it varies from year to year. When the leaves fall they carpet the temple yard, which is a sight in itself. In 2025 the tree was lit up from 21 to 24 November, from 5 to 8 pm. For this year's dates and how far the leaves have turned, call the Nagi Town tourist information centre on 0868-36-7311.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You really need a car. It's about 30 minutes from the Mimasaka interchange on the Chugoku Expressway, or 40 minutes from Tsuyama. Without one, the nearest station is Takano on the JR Kishin Line, then about 30 minutes by taxi. The last stretch is a narrow mountain road, so take it slowly.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The ginkgo is one of the biggest trees in Okayama Prefecture, with a trunk about 12 m round, and the best view is from near the main hall, where you can take in its whole height. The temple sits on the slopes below Mount Nagi. If you've come this far for autumn colour, [[tsuyama-castle]], whose stone walls are a cherry spot in spring, is in Tsuyama on the way back.</p>
`,

'lake-ikeda-rapeseed': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The rapeseed usually flowers from late December to early February, and January is the surest month. It's one of the earliest spring views in Japan: yellow flowers, the blue water of the lake and the cone of Mount Kaimon behind. The Ibusuki Nanohana Marathon in January is named after these flowers.</p>
<p>Check the weather. Kaimon's summit often sits in cloud, and the view needs the whole mountain, so go on a clear morning if you can.</p>
<h3>Where to stand</h3>
<p>Frame the flowers in front, the lake in the middle and Kaimon behind, and January light makes that easy. For a wider view, the golden torii on the north shore looks across the whole lake to the mountain. Lake Ikeda is the largest lake in Kyushu, a flooded volcanic crater, and it's famous for its giant eels.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>Ibusuki is best known for its sand baths, where you're buried in naturally heated sand on the beach. [[flower-park-kagoshima]] is also nearby, and it has flowers all through the winter. From Kagoshima city, the Ibusuki no Tamatebako sightseeing train runs to Ibusuki along the bay, and you can take a bus or taxi from there.</p>
`,

'yomitan-cosmos-field': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Peak moves around by a few weeks. In 2025 the field was almost in full bloom by 9 January, while in 2023 it was expected to peak in early February. Mid to late January is the safest time, but check local news or the Yomitan tourist association before you go.</p>
<p>It's a cosmos field in midwinter, which surprises most visitors. Okinawa is warm enough for cosmos, which flower in autumn on the mainland, to be sown in winter.</p>
<h3>The field</h3>
<p>The field sits on land that was once the US military's Yomitan Auxiliary Airfield. When it was returned, local businesses, village staff and junior high school volunteers planted the cosmos, about a million flowers in all. It's across the road from Yomitan Junior High School, in Zakimi.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Zakimi Castle, a World Heritage site with some of Okinawa's finest stone walls, is a few minutes' drive away, and the Yachimun no Sato pottery village is close by too. For a second cosmos field in the same trip, [[haneji-paddies-cosmos]] in Nago usually peaks a little later, at the end of January.</p>
`,

'haneji-paddies-cosmos': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The end of January is usually best. In 2024 the paddies were in full flower on 30 January, and the Haneji cosmos festival has been held on a weekend in mid to late January in past years, with flower picking for families. Don't leave it too late: in February the cosmos are ploughed back into the soil before the rice is planted.</p>
<h3>The paddies</h3>
<p>Haneji is one of the main rice-growing areas of northern Okinawa, and the cosmos are sown as green manure while the fields lie fallow. The planted area changes from year to year, from about 6.6 hectares to 10 at its largest. The fields are beside Route 58 at Kawakami, so they're easy to spot as you drive north from Naha.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>Nago is the gateway to northern Okinawa. Late January is also cherry blossom time here: [[nago-central-park]] has the early Okinawan cherries, and on the Motobu Peninsula there are more at [[mount-yae-cherry-forest-park]] and [[nakijin-castle-ruins-cherry-blossoms]]. You can see the cosmos and the cherries on the same day.</p>
`,

'tropical-dream-center': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Go for the Okinawa International Orchid Show, which is held here every January. The 39th show runs from 23 to 31 January 2027, from 8:30 am to 5:30 pm, with last entry at 5 pm. Adults pay 760 yen, and children of junior high age and under get in free. The centre closes on 1 February 2027 while the show is taken down.</p>
<p>Outside the show, the glasshouses are worth an hour on any visit to Ocean Expo Park. They keep orchids in flower all year among other tropical plants, and they're a good place to escape the heat or the rain.</p>
<h3>A new name</h3>
<p>In February 2026 the centre took the nickname Churashima Botanical Garden, and you'll see both names on signs and in the park's announcements. It's the same place, in the north of Ocean Expo Park, a short walk from the Churaumi Aquarium.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Late January is cherry time on the Motobu Peninsula. [[mount-yae-cherry-forest-park]] and [[nakijin-castle-ruins-cherry-blossoms]] are both under half an hour away by car, so you can pair the orchids with Okinawa's early cherries.</p>
`,

'yaese-park-cherry-blossoms': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late January to early February is the usual peak, and the town's cherry festival is held then. The 2027 dates aren't out yet, so check Yaese Town's website in January. The trees are lit up in the evenings during the festival. In past years the lights have been on from 6 to 9 pm.</p>
<p>Okinawa's cherries flower from north to south, the opposite of the mainland, because they need a cold spell to open, and the north of the island gets colder first. So Yaese, in the far south, is often a little later than Nago and Motobu.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The park climbs a hillside in Tomori, and the deep pink kanhizakura line the paths up the slope. During the festival there are food stalls, and it's a good chance to see an Okinawan town festival.</p>
<p>You'll want a car or a taxi. It's about 40 minutes' drive south of Naha. Pair it with [[yogi-park-cherry-blossoms]] in Naha, which usually peaks a week or two later.</p>
`,

'yogi-park-cherry-blossoms': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries are usually at their best in early to mid February, and in 2019 they lasted through to the end of the month. The Naha cherry festival is held here while they're out, and the park is free.</p>
<p>One thing to check first: Naha City is preparing to renovate the park, and a farewell festival was held there in February 2026 before the work. We couldn't find the dates for the work or what it means for the trees, so call the city's park section on 098-951-3239 before you plan a visit around it.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The kanhizakura line a stream that runs through the park, and their pink flowers hang over the water. They're a darker, bell-shaped cherry, quite different from the pale somei-yoshino of the mainland. The park dates from 1970 and sits in the middle of Naha, so it's easy to fit in between other plans.</p>
<p>For a bigger cherry day out, [[yaese-park-cherry-blossoms]] is about 40 minutes south by car, and the parks of the north, such as [[nago-central-park]], flower earlier, from late January.</p>
`,

'nomozaki-narcissus': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Go during the Nomozaki Narcissus Festival, usually held from late January to early February. In 2026 it ran from 31 January to 15 February. Entry is free, parking for about 350 cars is free too, and during the festival they've handed out bunches of narcissus to visitors.</p>
<p>Winter weather can cut the season short. In 2026 a snowstorm on 8 February did serious damage to the flowers, so check the city's bloom updates before a long trip.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The flowers grow on the Suisen no Oka, a hillside in the Nagasaki Nomozaki Dinosaur Park. Older guides call it Suisen no Sato. By bus, it's about 45 minutes from Nagasaki Station to the Kyoryu Park-mae stop, then a 5 minute walk. Some buses run only at weekends and on holidays, so check the timetable. By car, it's about 40 minutes from the Nagasaki interchange.</p>
<h3>The view</h3>
<p>The hill looks out over the East China Sea to Gunkanjima, the abandoned coal-mining island. On a clear day the scent of the narcissus, the blue sea and the island make this one of the best winter views in Kyushu. The dinosaur museum next door is good if you have children with you.</p>
`,

'kamegora-camellia-forest': `
<h3>Where it is</h3>
<p>The forest is on Hisaka Island, not on Fukue, the main island of the Goto chain. Boats from Fukue port take about 20 to 30 minutes. To get around the island you'll need a taxi or a hire car, and there's only one place to stay.</p>
<p>The camellias flower from January to March, and local people look after the trees. It's a pure stand of wild camellia, so you walk among old trunks rather than past planted rows.</p>
<h3>Make it a day on Hisaka</h3>
<p>Hisaka is one of the World Heritage sites of the Hidden Christians of Nagasaki, and the old Gorin church, a wooden church from the 1880s, is the island's best-known sight. Guided tours from Fukue port visit it in about two hours. The island's camellia forests are part of what earned its old village landscape protection.</p>
<p>During the Goto Camellia Festival, from late February to early March, there are guided walks into the wild camellia forests. For details, call the festival committee on 0959-72-2963. If you can't make it to Hisaka, the Goto Tsubaki Forest Park on Fukue, 5 minutes from the airport, has about 2,800 camellias of some 259 kinds.</p>
`,

'komuroyama-park-camellia-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The garden has something in flower from December to March, because it holds so many kinds. Mid January onwards is good, and the camellia show in late February and early March is the busiest time. In 2026 it ran from 21 February to 15 March, and it was the 39th. Each tree has a name tag, which makes the garden easy to enjoy even if you don't know one camellia from another.</p>
<h3>Up the mountain</h3>
<p>Mount Komuro is a small, grassy volcano cone. A single-seat chairlift runs from the park to the top, where there's a wide view of Sagami Bay, the Izu islands and, on a clear day, Mount Fuji. It's a good pairing with the garden. In spring, the slopes below are covered in about 100,000 azaleas, with an azalea festival in Golden Week.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Ito is an onsen town on the east coast of Izu. In March, [[izu-kogen-sakura-no-sato]] is a short drive south, at the foot of Mount Omuro, a bigger volcano cone with its own lift. The early cherries of the [[kawazu-river]] are further down the coast in February.</p>
`,

'anpachi-hyakubaien': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Anpachi Plum Festival runs from mid February to early March. In 2026 it was from 15 February to 8 March, and the 2027 dates should be out in January. With over 100 kinds of plum, the season is long: early kinds such as Kankobai and Tsuki no Katsura open in late January, and late ones such as Kaiunbai and Rinshibai last until late March. Even if the festival is cancelled, you can still walk round.</p>
<p>Entry is free. During the festival, stalls sell udon, mitarashi dango and zenzai, along with fresh vegetables from the town, and there's a show of bonsai plums.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's easiest by car, about 5 minutes from the Anpachi Smart interchange on the Meishin Expressway. For more plums, [[gifu-bairin-park]] in Gifu city is half an hour away, and in late April, the old wisteria of [[takehana-betsuin]] is in Hashima, next door.</p>
`,

'hofu-tenmangu-shrine': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The plum festival is held in mid to late February. In 2026 the city listed it from 15 February to 1 March, though the prefecture's site gave different dates, so check with the shrine (0835-23-7700) before you go. About 1,100 plum trees of some 16 kinds, including weeping plums, flower around the shrine. Past festivals have had children's dances and free amazake.</p>
<h3>The shrine</h3>
<p>Hofu Tenmangu enshrines Sugawara no Michizane, the scholar-poet who loved plum blossom, which is why so many Tenmangu shrines plant plums. It's counted with [[dazaifu-tenmangu-shrine]] and [[kitano-tenmangu-shrine]] as one of the three great Tenmangu, and it claims to be the first to have been built. Next to the main hall stands the Shunpuro, a wooden tower that's a registered cultural property, and it's worth climbing for the view over the grounds and the town.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a 15 minute walk north from JR Hofu Station, on the Sanyo Line between Hiroshima and Shimonoseki. The shrine is also a cherry spot in late March.</p>
`,

'shimizu-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The plum festival usually runs for about a month from early February. In 2026 it was from 7 February to 8 March. Sources disagree on the peak, from late February to mid March, which tells you something: with around 20 kinds, there's usually something in flower for most of the festival.</p>
<p>Many of the trees are old. Look out for Toji, an early white plum, double-flowered kinds such as Yae-kanbai, and Wachigai, a tree that carries red and white flowers on the same branches.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The plums grow in the plum garden of the First Park, the oldest part of the park, which began as a private garden. During the festival a food truck usually sells Japanese sweets and hot coffee. Entry is free, and only extras such as the field athletics course cost money. It's 10 minutes' walk from Shimizu-koen Station on the Tobu Urban Park Line.</p>
<p>Come back later in the year for the [[shimizu-park-april|azaleas and cherries]] in spring and the [[shimizu-park-november|maple valley]] in autumn.</p>
`,

'kasayama-camellia-grove': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Hagi Camellia Festival, from mid February to late March, is the best time. In 2026 it ran from 21 February to 22 March, and in 2027 it's planned for mid February to late March. During the festival, volunteer guides show visitors around the grove during the day.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Go at a weekend if you can. On Saturdays, Sundays and holidays during the festival, a free shuttle bus runs from the Koshigahama municipal car park to the grove. On weekdays there's no shuttle: from the Koshigahama bus stop it's about a 40 minute walk. Some car parks charge 300 yen at weekends.</p>
<h3>Around Kasayama</h3>
<p>Kasayama is a tiny volcano on a peninsula just outside Hagi, and a short path circles its crater, with views over the islands of the Sea of Japan. The camellias fill the wooded tip of the peninsula. Back in town, Hagi's old samurai quarter and the [[hagi-castle-ruins]] are an easy half day.</p>
`,

'cape-ashizuri-camellia-tunnel': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>February is the month. The camellias are usually at their best from early to late February, and the Ashizuri camellia festival is held then. The flowers are deep red and fall whole, so by late February the trail is often carpeted with them.</p>
<h3>The walk</h3>
<p>The nature trail runs about 1.5 km along the cliff top, and in places the camellias close over the path to make a tunnel. There are views out to the Pacific from the lookouts along the way. The cliffs are high and sheer, so stay inside the fences.</p>
<p>At the start of the cape stands Kongofuku-ji, temple 38 of the Shikoku 88-temple pilgrimage, and the white lighthouse at the tip is the classic photo. Allow two hours for the trail, the temple and the lighthouse.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Cape Ashizuri is remote. Buses from Nakamura Station on the Tosa Kuroshio Railway take about 1 hour 45 minutes, so most people drive or stay the night at one of the onsen hotels on the cape.</p>
`,

'kitanakagusuku-sunflowers': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The sunflower festival is usually held from mid February to early March, which makes it the earliest sunflower festival in Japan. The village calls it that, and on the mainland sunflowers don't flower until July. Most of what we found about it is a few years old, so check this year's dates with the village office on 098-935-2233 before you go.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 200,000 sunflowers, from small kinds to big ones, fill around 22,000 square metres of farmland in the Atta district, near the coast on the east side of the island. Plots in the field are taken by sponsors, who own their patch of sunflowers for the season.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Kitanakagusuku is in the middle of Okinawa Island, so it's an easy add to a drive between Naha and the north. Nakagusuku Castle, a World Heritage site with some of the best stonework in Okinawa, sits on the ridge above, next to the old Nakamura House. [[southeast-botanical-gardens]] in Okinawa City is about 20 minutes away.</p>
`,

'yodo-canal-kawazu-cherries': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The trees are usually in full bloom in early March. In 2026 they peaked around 7 March, and the local cherry festival was held on the weekend of 14 and 15 March. Kawazu cherries hold their flowers for a long time, so you've got a week or two either side of the peak.</p>
<p>If you're in Kyoto in early March, two to three weeks before the main cherry season, this is the place to see cherries.</p>
<h3>The walk</h3>
<p>About 200 trees line the canal for a few hundred metres, and their deep pink flowers are reflected in the water. It's about 10 minutes' walk from Yodo Station on the Keihan Main Line. The path is narrow, so on the festival weekend it gets crowded. Kyoto Racecourse is right next to the station, so avoid race days if you can.</p>
<p>Fushimi Inari is about 15 minutes away on the same Keihan line, and you pass the sake brewing district of Fushimi on the way. In late March and April, the cherries of [[daigo-ji-temple]] in Fushimi are some of the most famous in Kyoto.</p>
`,

'kasamatsu-kawazu-cherry-road': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Usually late February to early March. In 2026 the trees were 40 to 50 per cent open on 27 February, with full bloom expected in early March, and in 2025, a cold year, they were in full bloom on 17 March. Mie's tourism site posts bloom updates through the season, so check it before you go.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's much easier by car. There's free parking for about 100 cars in Kasasagi Farm Park and a temporary car park about 200 m east of it. By public transport, take the Suzunone bus on the Mikumo Matsusaka line from JR Matsusaka Station to the Mikumo regional office, then walk about 30 minutes.</p>
<h3>Why it's special</h3>
<p>Local people planted the trees along a farm canal, and that's how it still feels: a quiet path between fields, with pink trees on both banks and the flowers mirrored in the water. For our other spot in Matsusaka, the old [[kayumi-sasanqua]] flowers in early December.</p>
`,

'shirako-kawazu-cherries': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Shirako Onsen Cherry Festival runs from late February to early March. In 2026 it was from 21 February to 8 March. The trees start opening in mid February. Once they're about 30 per cent open, they're lit up from 6 to 9 pm, and the cherries glowing pink above the dark road are worth staying for.</p>
<h3>The cherries</h3>
<p>About 500 early-flowering cherries line the Kujukuri Beach Line, Route 30, from Hamajuku to the Nakazato beach road, and there are about 1,000 across the town. The Pacific is just behind the dunes.</p>
<h3>Stay the night</h3>
<p>Shirako is an onsen town best known for its tennis courts and sports camps, so there are plenty of hotels and it's an easy overnight stop. For the bus, take the Sotobo Line to Mobara, then a bus to Nakazato Kaigan. In March, another early cherry, the [[miura-kaigan-kawazu-cherries]], flowers on the other side of Tokyo Bay. For questions, the town's tourist association is on 0475-33-2117.</p>
`,

'minami-izu-cherry-and-rapeseed-festival': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The festival usually runs from early February to early March. Event listings give 1 February to 10 March for 2027, though the town hasn't confirmed it yet. The cherries are usually best from mid to late February. In 2026 the trees were lit up from 6 to 9 pm every evening, between the Maebara and Kujo bridges, and there were bamboo lantern evenings too.</p>
<p>It's free. The base is the Shimogamo Onsen Yu-no-Hana roadside station, with 200 parking spaces at 500 yen per car.</p>
<h3>Why go here and not Kawazu</h3>
<p>The [[kawazu-river]] festival up the coast is much more famous, with about 8,000 trees, and it gets very crowded. Minami-Izu has fewer trees, but more room to walk, and the rapeseed fields beside the river add yellow to the pink. The two flower at the same time, so you can see both in a day.</p>
<p>Shimogamo is an onsen village, so end the day with a bath. Shimoda is 20 minutes away by bus, and Yumigahama, one of the best beaches in Izu, is nearby.</p>
`,

'izu-kogen-sakura-no-sato': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Something is in flower from late September to May, but early March is the best time for a real show, when the early kinds are out, and late March to early April for somei-yoshino. In 2026 the park held night viewings from late March to early April, from 6 to 9 pm. It's free.</p>
<h3>The cherries</h3>
<p>About 1,000 trees of some 40 kinds grow on the lawns at the foot of Mount Omuro. Look for Izu's local cherries: Jogasaki-zakura, and kinds named after Ito itself, such as Ito-zakura and Ito-Komuro-zakura. The grass is open for picnics, and the scale of the trees against the volcano behind makes it one of the best hanami picnics in Izu.</p>
<h3>Up Mount Omuro</h3>
<p>Mount Omuro is a perfectly round grass-covered volcano, and a chairlift runs to the top. You can walk the rim of the crater in about 20 minutes, with views of Mount Fuji, the Izu islands and the sea. You can't climb it on foot, so the lift is the only way up. In the same area, [[komuroyama-park-camellia-garden]] is the place for camellias in winter.</p>
`,

'kannonyama-ume-no-sato': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Mid March is the usual peak, and the plum festival is held then. In 2026, visitors on 15 March found the slope in full flower, while those on 17 March thought it was just past its best, so the window is short. The festival can be cancelled depending on the year, but the garden itself is free and open whatever happens.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 3,000 plum trees, mostly the white Shirokaga with some red plums, cover a 4-hectare hillside. Because there are only four kinds, they tend to flower at once, which gives you a whole slope of white rather than a scattered show. This is one of the later plum spots near Tokyo, so it's a good choice if you've missed the February plums.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>There's parking for 100 cars. Without a car, it's about 30 minutes' walk from Ichihana Station on the Moka Railway, which runs steam trains on some weekends. In May, the [[ichikai-shibazakura-park]] in the same town is covered in pink moss phlox.</p>
`,

'akanuta-adonis-fields': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Shiga Fukujuso Festival runs in March. In 2026 it was from 7 to 22 March, from 10 am to 4 pm, and the peak is usually around the spring equinox, about 20 March. Entry is free, though parking is charged.</p>
<p>Go on a sunny day around midday. Adonis flowers only open fully in the sun, and close up when it's cloudy or cold, so a grey morning can leave you looking at a slope of shut buds.</p>
<h3>The colony</h3>
<p>About 500,000 plants grow wild on a 1.5-hectare slope, said to be the biggest colony in Nagano Prefecture. It's a city natural monument. The flowers are small and gold, and they often come up through the last of the snow, which is part of the charm.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>From JR Matsumoto Station, a Matsumoto Dentetsu bus on the Shiga line takes about 30 minutes. By car, it's about 11 km from the Azumino interchange on the Nagano Expressway. Matsumoto city, with [[matsumoto-castle]], is an easy add on the same day.</p>
`,

'nanaimai-kosaka-adonis': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The fukujuso festival runs through March, and in 2026 it was from 1 to 29 March. The flowers open one after another as the snow melts, so early March and late March can both be good, depending on the year. Go around midday on a sunny day, when the flowers are fully open.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>From the car park it's about a 500 m walk up a mountain path to the colony. It's steep in places, and the path can be muddy or icy in March, so wear proper shoes. The colony is in the Kosaka hamlet of Nanaimai, in the hills west of Nagano city, about 40 minutes by car from the Nagano interchange on the Joshinetsu Expressway. There's no practical public transport.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>More than 30,000 plants crowd one steep hectare, so the slope looks gold from top to bottom. Back in Nagano city, Zenko-ji is one of the most important temples in Japan and worth a few hours. If you'd like a second adonis colony, the [[akanuta-adonis-fields]] in Matsumoto are about an hour and a half's drive south.</p>
`,

'katakuri-no-sato': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Mid to late March is usually the peak. The katakuri flower festival in 2026 ran from 11 to 31 March. In 2025 the flowers were late and the festival was postponed, so check the Sano tourism office before you go (0283-27-3011). Like adonis, katakuri flowers only open in the sun, so pick a bright day and go from late morning.</p>
<h3>The slopes</h3>
<p>About 1.5 million katakuri, small purple lilies with swept-back petals, cover the north-facing woodland slopes of Mount Mikamo. A path runs through them, and you look up the slope at the flowers, which is the best way to see them.</p>
<p>Entry is free. Parking costs 500 yen per car in season.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Sano is famous for its ramen, so it's a good lunch stop. Mount Mikamo is in a large prefectural park with other walking trails. In late April and May, [[ashikaga-flower-park]], the big wisteria garden, is about 20 minutes away by car.</p>
`,

'numanotaira-adonis': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late March into April is the usual season, and the flowers open fully only from about 10 or 11 in the morning, and only in sunshine. A sunny late morning is the time to be there. The local village committee holds a fukujuso festival while the flowers are out. For this year's dates and how far the flowers have come, call the Yamato branch office of Kitakata City on 0241-38-3831.</p>
<h3>The colony</h3>
<p>Numanotaira has about a million plants, one of the largest adonis colonies in Japan. Local people describe it as a golden carpet, because the glossy petals catch the sun. Two walking paths wind through the slopes, and the Hidamari no Komichi path leading to Chosho-ji temple is lined with flowers too. The whole thing is run by the people of the hamlet, with help from former residents and a fan club.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 20 minutes by taxi from Yamato Station on the JR Ban'etsu West Line. Mountain roads in the area can be damaged after winter, so check the access before you set out. Kitakata, a short drive away, is famous for its ramen and its old storehouses.</p>
`,

'tsutsumi-plum-orchards': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The plums usually flower from mid March into early April, weeks after the plum season around Tokyo. They're fruit varieties such as Shirokaga and Nanko, so expect mostly white flowers rather than a garden mix of colours.</p>
<h3>Please be a good guest</h3>
<p>These are working orchards on private land, known locally as the Tsutsumi Ume Danchi, the Tsutsumi plum estate. You're free to look, but keep to the paths, don't touch the branches, and park only where you're allowed to. The fruit goes into local products: Ogawara sells a plum syrup called Ume Miyabi made from it.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 17 minutes by car from JR Ogawara Station. Ogawara is better known for the [[shiroishi-river-hitome-senbonzakura]], an 8 km line of cherry trees along the Shiroishi River that flowers in April, so you could come back a few weeks later for that.</p>
`,

'suizenji-jojuen': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries are usually at their best from late March to early April. In 2026 the Suizenji Jojuen festival was held on 28 and 29 March. The garden is lovely at any time of year, though, because it's built around a pond fed by clear spring water from the Aso volcano, which stays at the same temperature all year.</p>
<p>It's open from 8:30 am to 5 pm, with last entry at 4:30. Adults pay 400 yen, and children from primary school age to 15 pay 200 yen. A magnitude 7.1 earthquake struck the Kumamoto area on 28 July 2026, so check that the garden is open and its spring is flowing before you go (096-383-0074).</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The Hosokawa lords of Kumamoto laid out the garden from the 1630s, and it's a classic stroll garden: you walk round the pond, and the scenery changes as you go. The grass hill shaped like Mount Fuji is the best-known view. Inside the grounds stands Izumi Shrine, dedicated to the Hosokawa lords, and the Kokin Denju no Ma, an old teahouse moved here from Kyoto, where you can have matcha and a sweet looking out over the pond.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take the Kumamoto city tram to Suizenji Koen. It's about 20 minutes from the centre, and you can combine it with [[kumamoto-castle]] on the same day.</p>
`,

'saitobaru-burial-mounds': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The best time is late March to early April, when the cherries and rapeseed overlap. The rapeseed starts earlier, from mid March, and lasts into early April. The Saito Flower Festival runs while they're both out: in 2026 it was from 27 March to 5 April, with the cherries lit up in the evenings. On 4 April that year, the cherries were at full bloom and starting to fall.</p>
<h3>The mounds</h3>
<p>Saitobaru is one of the largest groups of ancient burial mounds in Japan, about 300 on a wide plateau above the town of Saito, built roughly 1,500 years ago. The view people come for is the round grassy mounds with yellow rapeseed and pink cherry around them. There are about 2,000 cherry trees and around 300,000 rapeseed plants.</p>
<p>The Saitobaru Archaeological Museum on the plateau explains what was found in the mounds and is worth an hour. Parking is free.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's under an hour by car from Miyazaki city. For more flowers in Miyazaki, [[florante-miyazaki]] is a big flower park near the city. In autumn, the plateau is covered in cosmos instead.</p>
`,

'hiroshima-botanical-garden': `
<h3>Why go for the begonias</h3>
<p>The begonia house keeps tuberous begonias in flower all year, so there's colour here whatever the season. It's one of several glasshouses, along with a big tropical house, a cactus house, a fuchsia house and a tropical water lily house. Across its 18 hectares the garden grows around 10,000 kinds of plant.</p>
<p>It's a good rainy day plan in Hiroshima, and a quiet change from the busy city centre and Miyajima.</p>
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>The garden opened in 1976 and sits in the hills on the western edge of the city. Buses run from the Hiroshima Bus Center in about 40 minutes, or you can change to a bus at Itsukaichi, which is quicker. Opening days, hours and fees have changed over the years, so check the garden's website or call 082-922-3600 before you go.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>For more flowers in Hiroshima, the garden of [[shukkeien-a-bombed-ginkgo|Shukkeien]] in the city centre is worth a visit, especially in late November when its ginkgo turns gold.</p>
`,

'higashi-village-azalea-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Higashi Village Azalea Festival runs through most of March. In 2026 it was from 1 to 22 March, from 9 am to 5 pm. Peak is usually in early to mid March, so azaleas here flower more than a month before Tokyo's. Entry was 500 yen for high school age and up in 2026 listings, and free for younger children, though an earlier year charged 300 yen. Parking for about 450 cars is free.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 50,000 azaleas of seven kinds cover a hillside in the village forest. Higashi is in Yanbaru, the forested north of Okinawa Island, which is now a World Natural Heritage site, so the garden is surrounded by subtropical forest. The Tsutsuji Eco Park next door has bungalows if you want to stay the night.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car. Higashi is on the quiet east coast, about two hours' drive from Naha. Combine it with a drive through Yanbaru, or with Nago, where [[nago-central-park]] has Okinawa's early cherries a month or so before. For questions, call the village office on 0980-43-2265.</p>
`,

'jindai-botanical-gardens-begonia-room': `
<h3>Why go</h3>
<p>Begonias flower here all year, so this is a good stop when little is out in Tokyo's parks, or when it's raining. The begonia room has big tuberous begonias in reds, pinks, yellows and whites, and hanging baskets overhead. They're much larger than most people expect a begonia to be.</p>
<p>The great greenhouse around it is worth the whole visit. There's a tropical water lily pond with dozens of kinds, orchids, and plants from tropical forests and dry lands, laid out so you walk through each in turn.</p>
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>Jindai Botanical Gardens is Tokyo's largest botanical garden, and adults pay 500 yen. It's closed on Mondays, or the next day when Monday is a holiday. Check the opening hours of the greenhouse before you go, because it closes earlier than the garden. Buses run from Chofu Station on the Keio Line.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Jindai-ji temple is right next to the garden, and the lanes around it are lined with soba shops; it's one of the best soba lunches in Tokyo. The garden itself has seasons on our calendar too: its big [[jindai-botanical-gardens|rose garden]] and the [[jindai-botanical-gardens-osmanthus|osmanthus]] in autumn.</p>
`,

'bizan-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Usually late March to early April. The cherries at the top flower a little later than those at the bottom, so if you're late for the city, go up. For about two weeks while they're out, the trees are lit up from sunset to 10 pm, though it's sometimes cancelled, so check first.</p>
<h3>The ropeway</h3>
<p>The ropeway starts on the fifth floor of the Awa Odori Kaikan, about 10 minutes' walk from JR Tokushima Station, and takes about six minutes to the top. Fares have gone up recently: a return is now 1,500 yen for junior high age and up, and 800 yen for primary school children. It runs from 9 am to 9 pm, closing at 5:30 pm from November to March. Cars hold up to 14 people, so expect a wait on spring weekends.</p>
<h3>Make an evening of it</h3>
<p>The top of Mount Bizan has a wide view over Tokushima city and the sea, and it's well known as a night view. Downstairs at the Awa Odori Kaikan, there's a daily performance of the Awa Odori, Tokushima's famous summer dance. Book a dance show, then ride up for the cherries and the city lights.</p>
`,

'asuwa-river-cherry-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The trees are usually at their best in the first week of April. The Fukui Cherry Festival runs through the season, and in 2026 it was extended to run from 21 March to 12 April because the blossom was late. The avenue is lit up from 6 to 10 pm during the Echizen Hana no Yoi, which ran from 27 March to 12 April in 2026.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>About 600 somei-yoshino form a 2.2 km tunnel along the river, one of the longest in Japan. Walk one bank and come back along the other. It's free and right in the middle of Fukui city, 10 to 15 minutes from the station.</p>
<p>Above the river, Asuwayama Park is covered in cherries too, with paper lanterns hung until 11 pm in the season. Atago-zaka, an old stone stairway up the hill, is lit with lanterns as well, and it was from 1 to 12 April in 2026.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Fukui is now on the Hokuriku Shinkansen, so it's easy to reach from Kanazawa. The temple of Eihei-ji and the dinosaur museum in Katsuyama are both an easy day trip from the city.</p>
`,

'ashino-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late April is usual, a little after [[hirosaki-park]]. The Kanagi Cherry Festival runs from mid to late April, with the trees lit up from sunset to about 9 pm. The exact dates depend on the blossom, so check with the Kanagi branch of Goshogawara City (0173-35-2111) before you go.</p>
<h3>The train through the blossom</h3>
<p>The picture people come for is the little Tsugaru Railway train passing through a tunnel of cherry blossom by Ashinokoen Station. The trains are infrequent, so check the timetable and wait at the line. Please stay off the tracks.</p>
<p>About 1,500 somei-yoshino and other cherries grow round the lake, one of the best cherry spots on the Tsugaru Peninsula.</p>
<h3>Osamu Dazai's home town</h3>
<p>Kanagi is where Osamu Dazai was born, and he's said to have loved this lake as a boy. His family's grand house, the Shayokan, is now a museum, a short ride or walk away in the centre of Kanagi. Come by train if you can: the Tsugaru Railway runs from Goshogawara, and Ashinokoen Station is right next to the park.</p>
`,

'echigo-hillside-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulip festival runs from mid April into Golden Week. In 2026 it was from 18 April to 6 May, from 9:30 am to 5 pm. Late April is usually the safest bet, since the tulips here flower a couple of weeks after Tokyo's. Adults pay 450 yen, over-65s 210 yen, and junior high age and under get in free. Parking is extra, 320 yen per car.</p>
<h3>The tulips</h3>
<p>About 160,000 bulbs of some 80 kinds are planted in curving bands along the slopes, so you see them in sweeps of colour rather than flat rows. Once the festival is over, the park lets visitors dig up bulbs to take home for a small fee; in 2026 that was on 16 May, at 100 yen.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from JR Nagaoka Station, on the Joetsu Shinkansen, in about 40 minutes. The park is big, with lawns, play areas and woodland, so it's an easy half day for families. For opening details, call the park centre on 0258-47-8001. In early April, [[takada-castle-ruins-park]] in Joetsu is one of the great cherry spots of Niigata.</p>
`,

'kitajima-tulip-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The park opens only for the tulip season, from late March to late April, 9 am to 5 pm. The tulips are usually at their best around 10 April. Entry is free and there's parking for 115 cars. It's closed for the rest of the year.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The town plants about 40,000 tulips each spring, so it's a neat, compact display rather than a field, and you can see it all in half an hour. For questions, call Kitajima Town on 088-698-9806.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Kitajima is just north of Tokushima city, a few minutes from the Matsushige Smart interchange. In early April, the cherries of [[bizan-park]] in Tokushima are in flower at the same time, and the Naruto whirlpools are about half an hour's drive north.</p>
`,

'hibiya-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The nemophila and tulips usually flower from late March into mid April. The beds are replanted in March, so the flowers come out all together. It's free and open all day.</p>
<h3>Why go</h3>
<p>This is the easiest place in central Tokyo to see nemophila. The famous blue hillsides at [[hitachi-seaside-park]] are two hours away by train, while Hibiya's beds sit beside the Shibaniwa lawn, two minutes from the subway. It's small, so treat it as a pleasant stop on a walk rather than a day out.</p>
<h3>Around the park</h3>
<p>Hibiya Park opened in 1903 as Tokyo's first Western-style park, and it's next to the Imperial Palace and a short walk from Ginza. It has more seasons on our calendar: the [[hibiya-park-kinmokusei|fragrant olive]] in early October and the old [[hibiya-park-kubikake-ginkgo|Kubikake ginkgo]] in late November. In late March, the cherries of [[chidorigafuchi]] are a 20 minute walk away round the palace moat.</p>
`,

'aonodomon': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The nemophila are usually at their best from mid April to early May. Morning light brings out the contrast between the blue flowers and the pale rock of the cliffs. In the evening, the cliffs throw long shadows over the valley.</p>
<h3>The tunnel</h3>
<p>Aonodomon is a tunnel dug by hand through the cliffs of Kyoshuho by a monk called Zenkai in the 18th century, to give travellers a safe way past a deadly cliff path. The story is told in Kikuchi Kan's short story <em>Onshu no Kanata ni</em>, and you can still see the chisel marks inside. Parts of the old road through it are now closed to cars and open only on foot, so if you're taking photos with a tripod, leave room for people to pass.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>The nemophila grow on the far bank of the Yamakuni River, facing the cliffs. Aonodomon is at the mouth of the Yabakei gorge, and in November the [[yabakei-gorge]] is one of Kyushu's best autumn leaf drives. Parking at the tunnel is free.</p>
`,

'flower-village-kamu-no-sato': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The nemophila and moss phlox hill opens from late March to early May. In 2026 it ran from 28 March to 10 May, 9 am to 5 pm, with last entry at 4:30, and opening hours were longer over the Golden Week holidays. Mid to late April is usually the best time to see both flowers at once.</p>
<p>The fee changes with the flowers: adults paid between 700 and 1,200 yen in 2026, and children aged 4 to primary school between 300 and 600 yen. You pay less early and late in the season and more at peak.</p>
<h3>The hill</h3>
<p>About a million nemophila and 300,000 moss phlox cover four hectares of hillside, one of the biggest nemophila displays in western Japan. The garden opens again later in the year with different flowers: hydrangeas and hollyhocks in June, and kochia and cosmos from late August to October.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>It's run by the same company as [[sera-kogen-farm-april|Sera Kogen Farm]], whose tulip festival is on at the same time, nearby. Sera is a plateau of farms and flower parks in the middle of Hiroshima Prefecture, and you'll need a car.</p>
`,

'japan-mint-cherry-passage': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Mint opens the lane for one week in mid April, when its late double-flowered cherries are at their best. In 2026 it was from 9 to 15 April. Entry is free, but you have to book a time slot online in advance, first come first served. In 2026 bookings opened on 18 March at 9 am, and the slots went quickly. The 2027 dates should be announced around February or March.</p>
<p>In 2026 entry ran from 10 am on weekdays and 9 am at weekends, until 7:15 pm. The evening slots are worth booking, because the trees are lit up after dark.</p>
<h3>The cherries</h3>
<p>About 340 trees of some 140 kinds line the 560 m lane, and most are kinds you rarely see elsewhere, such as Kanzan, with its heavy pink double flowers. Each tree has a name board. The Mint chooses a cherry of the year each spring and puts it in the spotlight.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>The Mint stands on the bank of the [[okawa-river]], whose somei-yoshino flower about two weeks earlier, and [[osaka-castle-park]] is across the river. It's about 15 minutes' walk from Temmabashi Station.</p>
`,

'asahi-funakawa-spring-quartet': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The four layers line up in early to mid April, when the cherries are at their peak: pink cherries, yellow rapeseed and tulips beneath them, and the snowy peaks of the Northern Alps behind. In 2026 the event ran from 3 to 16 April, with the trees lit up from sunset to about 9 pm.</p>
<p>The snow on the mountains matters as much as the flowers, so pick a clear day, when the peaks stand out sharply behind the trees.</p>
<h3>Parking and getting there</h3>
<p>Parking now has to be booked online in advance, at 1,500 yen per car, and a car park beside the river is only open on weekdays. Shuttle buses run from the temporary car parks. By train, take the Ainokaze Toyama Railway to Tomari Station, then a temporary bus that runs only during the event, about 10 minutes. For details, call the Asahi tourist association on 0765-83-2780.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Asahi is at the eastern end of Toyama Prefecture, close to Niigata. Later in April, the [[tonami-tulip-park]] in western Toyama holds one of Japan's biggest tulip festivals.</p>
`,

'shirakimine-kogen': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>In spring, the rapeseed is usually at its best from late March to mid April, when the cherries flower alongside it. In autumn, about 200,000 cosmos flower from late September into October, and in 2026 they were still expected in mid October to early November.</p>
<h3>The hill</h3>
<p>About 100,000 rapeseed plants cover the hilltop, with cherries along the edges and a wide view over Omura Bay. The yellow and pink with blue water behind make one of the best spring views in Nagasaki Prefecture. The Cosmos Hanauchukan, the visitor centre on the hill, can tell you how far the flowers have come: call 0957-23-9003.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 20 minutes by car from JR Isahaya Station, up a winding road. For more cherries the same week, [[omura-park]] in Omura, a short drive north, is one of Kyushu's top cherry spots.</p>
`,

'takehana-betsuin': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Mino Takehana Wisteria Festival runs in the second half of April. In 2026 it was from 17 to 29 April, from 10 am to 4 pm, and entry was free. The wisteria is usually in full bloom around the last week of April. Under the trellis there's a matcha corner and food stalls.</p>
<p>If you can, go on 3 May for the Takehana Festival, when the town's old festival floats are pulled through the streets. In 2026, seven floats were pulled and the other five were on show in the town.</p>
<h3>The wisteria</h3>
<p>It's a single tree, said to be more than 300 years old, that spreads out to cover a whole trellis by itself. It's a natural monument of Gifu Prefecture.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a 5 minute walk from Hashima-Shiyakusho-mae Station on the Meitetsu Takehana Line, or about 5 minutes by car from the Gifu-Hashima interchange on the Meishin Expressway. There's free temporary parking during the festival. For questions, call the Hashima tourist association on 058-322-2303. In February and March, the [[anpachi-hyakubaien]] plum garden is right next door in Anpachi.</p>
`,

'uminonakamichi-seaside-park-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The nemophila usually peak from early to late April, during the Uminonakamichi Flower Picnic, the park's spring flower festival. In 2026 it ran from 20 March to 17 May, from 9:30 am to 5:30 pm, with last entry at 4:30. Adults pay 450 yen, over-65s 210 yen, and junior high age and under get in free.</p>
<h3>The flowers</h3>
<p>About a million nemophila cover the Flower Hill, with the park's Ferris wheel rising above them. Tulips and other spring flowers are planted around the park at the same time.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Uminonakamichi Station on the JR Kashii Line is right next to the park, about half an hour from Hakata. You can also come by ferry across Hakata Bay from the city, which is a nice way to arrive. The park is huge, so hire a bike at the gate if you want to see more than the flowers. In autumn, the [[uminonakamichi-seaside-park|same park]] is covered in cosmos, and in spring, [[nokonoshima-island-park]] across the bay has its own flower fields.</p>
`,

'ushijima-wisteria-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The garden opens only while the wisteria flowers, roughly mid April to early May. In 2026 it was open from 11 April to 3 May. Peak is usually mid to late April, but it moves with the weather, so check the garden's bloom updates before you go. The fee has been 1,200 yen for adults and 600 yen for children aged 4 to primary school, though it may change, and it pays for the upkeep of the tree.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The great wisteria is put at about 1,200 years old, and its longest flower clusters reach about 2 m. It's the only wisteria in Japan designated a Special Natural Monument, the highest rank there is. The clusters hang over a pond, and on a still day the reflection doubles them.</p>
<p>Go on a weekday morning if you can. The garden is compact, and the space under the trellis fills up at weekends.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes' walk from Fuji-no-Ushijima Station on the Tobu Urban Park Line, a station named after the tree. From central Tokyo, allow about an hour. Our [[ushijima-wisteria]] page has more on the garden.</p>
`,

'towada-kanchogai-street': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries are usually at their best from mid April into early May. The Towada Spring Festival runs through the season: in 2026 it was planned for 18 April to 5 May, but the blossom came early, so the evening lights and the City Hall viewing terrace opened from 10 April. The trees are lit up from 6 to 9 pm. Watching is free.</p>
<p>The highlight is the Sakura Yabusame, horseback archery under the blossom, with women riders in bright costumes galloping down the avenue. Check the festival site for the day it's held.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>Kanchogai-dori, also called the Koma Kaido, is 1.1 km long and 36 m wide, lined with about 156 cherries and around 160 pines, and it's on the list of Japan's 100 best roads. The Towada Art Center stands on the avenue, with outdoor artworks along the pavement, so the street is worth a walk at any time of year.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Towada is the gateway to Lake Towada and the [[oirase-gorge]], whose stream is at its best in autumn. For more cherries, [[hirosaki-park]] is about two hours away by car. For festival details, call the Towada Oirase tourism office on 0176-24-3006.</p>
`,

'tsutsujigaoka-park-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak in early to mid April. In 2026 full bloom was forecast for 7 to 10 April, and the park's cherry festival ran from 26 March to 19 April. The weeping cherries open a few days before the somei-yoshino, so the season here lasts a little longer than at most parks.</p>
<h3>The cherries</h3>
<p>About 350 trees, including weeping, somei-yoshino, double-flowered and higan cherries. The history goes back to 1695, when the Sendai lord Date Tsunamura built a Buddhist hall here and planted higan and weeping cherries in its grounds. One weeping cherry from that planting survives and is protected by the city.</p>
<p>It's a popular picnic spot, with food stalls during the festival, and it gets busy on weekend evenings. The park's car park is closed in the cherry season, so come by train.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's 5 minutes' walk from Tsutsujigaoka Station on the JR Senseki Line, one stop from Sendai, or about 15 minutes' walk from Sendai Station itself. For a cherry day trip from Sendai, the [[shiroishi-river-hitome-senbonzakura]] is about 50 minutes south by train.</p>
`,

'kyoto-botanical-gardens-begonia-show': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tuberous begonia show is held in spring in the conservatory's Jungle Room, with some 250 pots on display. In 2025 it ran until 6 April, overlapping with the cherries outside. The 2027 dates will be on the garden's website nearer the time.</p>
<p>Entry to the garden is 500 yen for adults and 250 yen for over-65s and high school students, and junior high age and under get in free. The conservatory is included in that price, and it's open from 10 am to 4 pm, with last entry at 3:30.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Kyoto Botanical Gardens opened in 1924, and it's the oldest public botanical garden in Japan. The begonias are a good excuse to see the conservatory, one of the largest in the country, but in early April the main event is outside: the garden's cherries, from early weeping trees to late double-flowered ones. See our [[kyoto-botanical-gardens]] page for those.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Kitayama Station on the Karasuma subway line is next to the north gate. The south gate opens onto the Kamo River, and a walk down the riverbank to Shimogamo Shrine takes about 30 minutes.</p>
`,

'shimizu-park-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Two seasons in one month. The cherry festival runs from mid March to early April, and in 2026 it was from 14 March to 5 April, with the trees lit up in the evening. Then the azalea festival follows from mid April into Golden Week: in 2026 it ran from 18 April to 6 May. The azaleas are usually at their best in late April and early May.</p>
<h3>The flowers</h3>
<p>The park has about 2,000 cherry trees of some 50 kinds, so there's a long season from early kinds to late double-flowered ones. The azaleas number about 20,000 plants of around 100 kinds, including yama-tsutsuji and mochi-tsutsuji, and some old ones are said to be about 300 years old.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Entry is free, and you only pay for extras such as the big field athletics course, which is popular with families. It's 10 minutes' walk from Shimizu-koen Station on the Tobu Urban Park Line. The same park has the [[shimizu-park|plum garden]] in February and the [[shimizu-park-november|maple valley]] in late autumn.</p>
`,

'okinawa-comprehensive-athletic-park-deigo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Okinawa's deigo flower from March to May, and in this park they're often at their best in April. The timing varies a lot: in some years the trees are covered in red, and in others they flower late or only lightly. Ask locals and you'll hear an old saying that a heavy deigo year means a bad typhoon season.</p>
<p>The fallen flowers are part of the show. When they drop onto the grass, the lawns turn into what local papers call a red carpet, so a day or two after peak can be the best time to visit.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The park straddles Okinawa City and Kitanakagusuku, on the east coast, and it's planted with about 197,000 subtropical trees of 88 kinds. It's mostly a sports park, with the prefectural athletics stadium and wide lawns, so it's a quiet place to see the deigo away from the tourist sights. Entry to the park is free.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>In February, the [[kitanakagusuku-sunflowers]] flower in the farmland just up the coast, and [[southeast-botanical-gardens]] in Okinawa City is about 15 minutes away by car.</p>
`,

'miyakojima-tropical-botanical-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The deigo avenue is usually at its reddest in spring, but sources disagree on when: some say February to March, others April to May. The trees also vary a lot from year to year. If the deigo is the reason for your trip, call the garden on 0980-73-2690 before you book.</p>
<p>The rest of the garden is in flower all year, with bougainvillea and hibiscus, so it's worth a visit whatever the deigo is doing.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Entry is free, and the garden is open every day from 8:30 am to 6 pm, with hours that vary a little by season. The deigo avenue is about 400 m long, and when the trees flower it becomes a tunnel of red. The garden is a large, shady park, a good break from the sun between beaches.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes by car from Miyako Airport, and there's parking. Miyako has little public transport, so most visitors hire a car for the island.</p>
`,

'cape-mikanzaki-lilies': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wild Easter lilies usually flower from late March into April, with early to mid April the safest window. Some years they come early or run into May. They grow wild on the grassy slopes of the headland, and pink evening primrose flowers among them at the same time.</p>
<h3>The cape</h3>
<p>Cape Mikanzaki is on the west coast of Ishigaki, and its white lighthouse, first lit in 1983, stands above steep cliffs. The cape faces west, so it's one of the island's best sunset points, and the lilies glow in the low evening light. It's windy up here, and the steps to the lighthouse are steep, so hold on to hats and children. Drones are banned.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car or a taxi, and it's about 40 minutes from Ishigaki town. Combine it with Kabira Bay, Ishigaki's most famous view, also on the west side of the island. If you're on the island in summer, the night-flowering [[iriomote-sagaribana]] on the next island is a different kind of flower trip.</p>
`,

'hamura-tulip-fields': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulip festival runs for about ten days in early to mid April. In 2026 it was from 5 to 15 April, from 10 am to 5 pm, and entry was free. The tulips usually peak around the middle of the festival. Before that, the same area holds a cherry festival along the Tama River embankment, and in early April you can sometimes catch the last cherries and the first tulips together.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 350,000 tulips fill the Negarami-mae paddies, one of the biggest tulip displays in the Kanto region. After the festival the fields are flooded and planted with rice, so the tulips are a spring crop in a working paddy.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Walk from Hamura Station on the JR Ome Line, about 20 minutes. Temporary parking is limited and the roads nearby get jammed at weekends, so take the train. At busy weekends, local shops set up stalls. For questions, call Hamura City's industry promotion section on 042-555-1111.</p>
`,

'yokohama-satoyama-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The garden opens for its spring festival from late March to early May. In 2026 it ran from 19 March to 6 May, from 9:30 am to 4 pm, and entry was free. The tulips are usually at their best in the first half of April, then other flowers take over the big bed into May.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The big flower bed covers about 10,000 square metres of a wooded hillside, and in 2026 it held about 200,000 plants of some 150 kinds, mostly tulips. It's part of Garden Necklace Yokohama, the city's spring flower festival. The garden also opens for an autumn festival, so check before you go at other times of year.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take a bus from Nakayama Station on the JR Yokohama Line or the Green Line to Jichikaikan-mae, then walk about 10 minutes. It's next to Zoorasia, one of Japan's best zoos, so it's an easy day out with children. For the city-centre tulips of the same festival, see [[yokohama-park]].</p>
`,

'kiso-sansen-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulip festival runs from late March to mid April. The park's own announcement for 2026 gave 28 March to 19 April, though another listing had shorter dates, so check with the park centre (0584-54-5531) for next year's. Early April is usually the peak. Entry to the park is free, and it's open from 9:30 am to 5:30 pm.</p>
<h3>The flower bed</h3>
<p>The giant bed at the Park Center is planted with over 200,000 tulips and blue muscari in patterns that are best seen from above. The observation tower, which costs 630 yen for adults, gives the classic view, and it also looks out over the three rivers. Check that it's open, because one listing showed it as closed.</p>
<p>In winter, the park grows ice tulips, bulbs chilled to flower out of season, which are usually in bloom in January and February.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>The park is in Kaizu, in the flat delta land of southwest Gifu. In late February, the [[anpachi-hyakubaien]] plum garden is a short drive north, and later in April the [[takehana-betsuin]] wisteria is in Hashima.</p>
`,

'sera-kogen-farm-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulip festival runs from mid April into Golden Week. In 2026 it was from 10 April to 6 May, and in 2025 it was extended to 11 May. The tulips are usually best in the second half of April. The fee changes with the flowers, and at peak in 2026 it was 1,200 yen for adults and 600 yen for children aged 4 to primary school.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 750,000 tulips of some 200 kinds are planted in stripes and patterns across 6.5 hectares of hillside, one of the biggest tulip festivals in western Japan. The farm is on a plateau in the middle of Hiroshima Prefecture, so the flowers come out a little later than in the city.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>The same company runs [[flower-village-kamu-no-sato]], whose nemophila and moss phlox hill is in flower at the same time. The farm opens again in summer for its sunflowers, see our [[sera-kogen-farm]] page. You'll need a car: Sera is about 20 minutes from the Sera interchange.</p>
`,

'michinoku-lakeside-park-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The spring flower festival runs from early April to Golden Week. In 2026 it was from 4 April to 6 May, with events mainly at weekends. The tulips are usually at their best from mid to late April, when about 1,400 cherry trees, pansies, violas and rapeseed are also in flower. Adults pay 450 yen, over-65s 210 yen, and junior high age and under get in free. Parking costs 400 yen.</p>
<h3>The tulips</h3>
<p>The Irodori Hiroba bed covers about 7,000 square metres, and in 2025 it held about 90,000 tulips of 85 kinds. It's Tohoku's only national government park, on the shore of Lake Kamafusa, so there's plenty of space beyond the flowers, with lawns and a village of old farmhouses moved here from around the region.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The park is in Kawasaki, about 40 minutes by car from Sendai. It's open from 9:30 am to 5 pm from March to October. For opening details, call the park centre on 0224-84-5991. In October, the [[michinoku-lakeside-park|same park]] is known for its red kochia.</p>
`,

'yukiyagawa-dam-forest-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Forest, Water and Tulip Festival runs from late April to mid May, and the tulips are usually at their best in the first half of May. That's later than almost anywhere else on Honshu, because Karumai is in the cold hills of northern Iwate. On festival weekends there are stalls and stage events, and on stage-event days a free shuttle bus runs from the town centre.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 150,000 tulips fill fields beside the lake behind the Yukiyagawa Dam. The park also has a campsite and cabins, so you can make a night of it.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's easiest by car. By public transport, a JR bus from Ninohe Station takes about an hour. For this year's festival dates, call the park on 0195-45-2444.</p>
`,

'sarakimisaki-tulip-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulips are usually at their best from late April to mid May, and the tulip fair is held in May. This is Hokkaido, so they flower a month after Tokyo's.</p>
<h3>The cape</h3>
<p>About 50,000 tulips of some 60 kinds grow around a Dutch-style windmill on the cape. The Kanrin Maru, the ship that carried Japan's first embassy across the Pacific to America in 1860, ran aground off this cape in 1871, and there's a monument to it. The ship was built in the Netherlands, hence the windmill. Local volunteers dig up the bulbs in summer and replant them in autumn every year.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 15 minutes by car from Kikonai Station on the Hokkaido Shinkansen. For this year's dates, call the Kikonai tourist association on 01392-6-7357. Hakodate is about an hour away, and [[goryokaku-park]] there is one of Hokkaido's best cherry spots in late April.</p>
`,

'yokosuka-iris-garden-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria is usually at its best from late April to mid May, and the garden holds a wisteria festival over Golden Week. In 2025 it ran from 19 April to 5 May. The garden charges entry in the flower seasons, and fees change, so check its website before you go.</p>
<h3>The wisteria</h3>
<p>About 200 wisteria of 13 kinds grow on trellises and in a walk-through tunnel, in white and shades of purple. It's a quieter wisteria spot than the famous ones, and it's worth it for the tunnel alone.</p>
<h3>Come back in June</h3>
<p>The garden is best known for its irises, and the iris festival runs from late May to late June. In 2026 it was from 23 May to 21 June. See our [[yokosuka-iris-garden]] page for that. To get there, take a Keikyu bus from JR Kinugasa Station to Shobuen, then walk 2 minutes. For questions, call the garden on 046-853-3688.</p>
`,

'mifuneyama-rakuen-april': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The spring flower festival covers the azaleas, the great wisteria and the fresh spring maples. In 2026 it ran from 6 April to 6 May, from 8 am to 6:30 pm. The wisteria usually peaks in late April, with the azaleas at the same time. Adults paid 700 yen and primary school children 450 yen.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The great wisteria grows beside the Haginoo teahouse, and it's put at about 170 years old. The azalea valley below it has about 200,000 Kurume and Hirado azaleas, which cover the slope in pink and red. Behind it all rise the cliffs of Mount Mifune. The garden was laid out in the 1840s by the lord of Takeo.</p>
<h3>Other seasons</h3>
<p>The garden is also one of Kyushu's best autumn leaf spots, with evening light-ups: the autumn festival runs from 6 November to 6 December in 2026. Our [[mifuneyama-rakuen]] page has more on the azalea season. Takeo is an old onsen town, and Takeo-Onsen Station is about 12 minutes away by bus. For questions, call the garden on 0954-23-3131.</p>
`,

'chausuyama-highlands': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Chausuyama Moss Phlox Festival runs from mid May to early June, a month after the lowland moss phlox. In 2026 it was from 9 May to 7 June, from 9 am to 4:30 pm. The hill was lit up on a few weekend evenings in May. Late May is usually the peak.</p>
<p>Entry is free. The sightseeing lift up to the flowers costs 1,000 yen return or 800 yen one way, and it takes about 13 minutes each way. On fine weekends the queue gets long, so you can walk up instead, about 1 km up the ski slope, 30 to 40 minutes.</p>
<h3>The hill</h3>
<p>About 400,000 moss phlox cover the top of Mount Hagitaro, next to Mount Chausu, the highest peak in Aichi. The flowers are planted in pink, white and purple bands, and on a clear day you can see the Southern Alps from the top.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car. It's about two hours from Nagoya, and the ski area car park is free, with space for about 1,000 cars, though it can still fill at weekends. For details, call the Chausuyama Kogen association on 0536-87-2345.</p>
`,

'tottori-hanakairo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Something is in flower all year, but the lilies are the reason to come, and they peak from June into July. The 2026 lily and hydrangea festival had about 80,000 lilies of some 80 kinds in flower. In winter, the glass dome and the evening illuminations are the draw.</p>
<p>The fee changes by season. During the 2026 autumn festival, adults paid 960 yen and primary and junior high school children 480 yen, while summer evenings after 5 pm were cheaper. Check the park's website for the season you're visiting.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Tottori Hanakairo is one of the largest flower parks in Japan, at the foot of Mount Daisen, the tallest mountain in the Chugoku region. A 1 km covered walkway circles the park, so you can see most of it even in rain, and the 21 m glass Flower Dome keeps tropical plants and flowers going through the winter.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Shuttle buses run from JR Yonago Station in about 25 minutes. Yonago is the base for Daisen and for the Adachi Museum of Art, whose garden is often ranked the best in Japan. For questions, call the park on 0859-48-3030.</p>
`,

'takino-suzuran-hillside-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tulips are usually at their best from mid May into early June, a month after Tokyo's. The park's Tulip and Lily of the Valley Festa ran from 16 to 31 May in 2026, and the local paper reported the peak lasting until the end of May. Adults pay 450 yen, over-65s 210 yen, and under-15s get in free. Parking costs extra.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 230,000 tulips of some 120 kinds fill the Country Garden, planted in two hillside sections, the Irodori no Oka and the Niji no Oka, in stripes and patterns of colour. The park is named after the lily of the valley, suzuran, a flower closely tied to Hokkaido, which flowers around the same time.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from Makomanai Station, the southern end of the Namboku subway line. The park is huge and hilly, with waterfalls in the woods and a big children's area. In October it has autumn leaves, see [[takino-suzuran-hillside-park-october]].</p>
`,

'gifu-world-rose-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The spring Rose Festival runs from mid May into early June. In 2026 it was from 9 May to 7 June, from 9 am to 5 pm, and the roses are usually best in the second half of May. Adults paid 1,500 yen in May and less in June, and high school age and under got in free. There's a second, smaller festival in autumn, from October into November.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>This is the old Hana Festa Memorial Park, renamed, and it's one of the biggest rose gardens in the world, with thousands of kinds. It's spread over a large hilly park, so you can easily spend half a day. During the festival there are guided tours with rose experts.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's in Kani, about an hour's drive from Nagoya, and most visitors come by car. Check the garden's website for buses during the festival. In the same weeks, [[nakanoshima-park-rose-garden]] in Osaka and [[keisei-rose-garden]] near Tokyo are also at their best.</p>
`,

'sakura-kusabue-no-oka-rose-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The roses are usually best in the middle of May. Many of the roses here are old roses and wild species, which flower only once a year, so don't leave it late: a visitor on 25 May 2024 found most already past their best.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 1,250 kinds and 2,500 plants, mostly wild species and old roses, grown for their history as much as their looks. In 2015 the garden won the Award of Garden Excellence from the World Federation of Rose Societies. The scent on a warm May morning is the best thing about it.</p>
<p>Entry was 410 yen for adults in 2024. The garden is part of Kusabue-no-Oka, a big park with a mini railway (extra charge), so it's good with children.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Sakura is an old castle town with a samurai district and the National Museum of Japanese History. In April, the tulips by the Dutch windmill at [[sakura-furusato-hiroba]] are the town's big spring draw. For a modern rose garden near Tokyo, [[keisei-rose-garden]] in Yachiyo is about 30 minutes away.</p>
`,

'hamadera-park-rose-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The garden has two seasons: spring, from early May to early June, and autumn, from October into November. Mid May is usually the peak. Entry is free, and the rose garden is open from mid March to mid December, from 10 am to 4:30 pm. It closes on Tuesdays, or the next day when Tuesday is a holiday.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Sources put it at about 6,000 to 6,500 plants of 300 to 500 kinds, so it's a serious collection, and the fact that it's free makes it one of the best-value flower spots in Osaka. Hamadera Park itself is one of the oldest parks in Japan, opened in 1873, and it's famous for the old pine forest along the shore of Osaka Bay.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's 5 to 8 minutes' walk from Hamaderakoen Station on the Nankai Main Line, about 20 minutes from Namba. The old wooden station building, from 1907, is worth a look. Weekends get busy, especially at peak. For more roses in the city, [[nakanoshima-park-rose-garden]] is right in central Osaka.</p>
`,

'ina-town-memorial-park-rose-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The rose festival runs through May, and the roses are usually best from mid May into early June. There's a second season from late October to mid November. In 2026, entry was 500 yen in the paid season, from 9 am to 6 pm, and free for 18 and under. Outside the paid season the garden is free.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 5,000 plants of some 400 kinds on 1.4 hectares. It's one of the biggest rose gardens in Saitama, and less crowded than the famous ones in Tokyo, so it's a good choice for a relaxed afternoon with the roses.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Ina is just north of Omiya, and the New Shuttle line runs from Omiya Station into the town. In the same month, [[yono-park]] in Saitama city has another rose garden.</p>
`,

'ogata-village-nanohana-road': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries and rapeseed usually flower together in the second half of April, and the village holds its Cherry and Rapeseed Festival then. In 2026 it ran from 18 April to 5 May, near the Hotel Sun Rural Ogata, with a mini train and rapeseed picking. Peak can move into early May in a cold year, so check with the village office (0185-45-3653) before a long trip.</p>
<h3>The road</h3>
<p>About 4,000 cherry trees, somei-yoshino, double cherries and beni-yamazakura, line about 11 km of Prefectural Route 298, with rapeseed planted all the way along beneath them. Ogata is built on land reclaimed from Lake Hachirogata in the 1960s, so it's completely flat, with the road running straight to the horizon. It's best seen by car or by bike.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Ogata is about 10 minutes by car from Hachirogata Station on the JR Ou Line. Parking along the road is limited, around 50 spaces on the verge, and it's busy at weekends. In the same weeks, [[kakunodate-samurai-district]] has some of Tohoku's most famous weeping cherries.</p>
`,

'mount-tokusenjo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Mid May is usually the peak. In 2026 the azaleas at the trailhead were in full bloom on 17 May, while the first viewpoint was about 70 per cent open and the summit only 50 per cent. By 23 May they were nearly over. The flowers open from the bottom up, so if the lower slopes are past their best, keep climbing.</p>
<h3>The mountain</h3>
<p>About 500,000 wild mountain azaleas and Japanese azaleas, yama-tsutsuji and renge-tsutsuji, cover some 50 hectares of the hill. They grow wild rather than in a garden, so the whole slope turns orange and red. The walk from the trailhead to the summit is short but uphill, so wear proper shoes.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 30 minutes by car from JR Kesennuma Station. Parking is free, and there's a toilet at the trailhead, but no vending machines, so bring water. At peak weekends, come early. A donation box helps pay for the upkeep.</p>
`,

'kechimyaku-zakura': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries of Matsumae usually flower from late April into mid May, and the Matsumae Cherry Festival runs through the season: in 2026 it was from 18 April to 10 May. The Kechimyaku-zakura is a Nanden cherry, a mid-season kind, so it's usually at its best in early May.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The tree stands in the grounds of Kozen-ji, a temple in the temple district just north of Matsumae Castle. It's over 300 years old and counted among the three great trees of Matsumae. It's the parent of the Nanden cherry, the town's most common kind. Its name comes from a local legend about a young woman and a Buddhist blood-lineage scroll, which saved the tree from being cut down.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Matsumae is Hokkaido's only castle town, with some 250 kinds and over 10,000 cherry trees. It's a long trip: buses from Kikonai Station, on the Hokkaido Shinkansen, take about 90 minutes to the Matsushiro stop, then it's a 10 minute walk. The temple has no parking. See [[matsumae-park]] for the rest of the town's cherries.</p>
`,

'nagareyama-open-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The open garden days are held over three days in May, in rose season, and the dates are announced each spring. The city publishes a map of the gardens taking part. Most are private homes, so the gardens are only open on those days, and there's no booking. It's free.</p>
<h3>What it's like</h3>
<p>This is a chance to walk into Japanese suburban gardens you'd never otherwise see, many of them planted with roses, and to meet the people who made them. It's run by a local gardening club, Karento, started by people who entered the city's gardening contest, and it draws thousands of visitors. In 2016, around 20,000 people came.</p>
<p>Be a good guest: stay on the paths, ask before taking photos of people, and remember that you're in someone's home.</p>
<h3>Getting around</h3>
<p>The gardens are spread across the city, from Minami-Nagareyama to Nagareyama-otakanomori stations, both on the Tsukuba Express, about 25 minutes from Akihabara. Pick a cluster of gardens on the map and walk between them, or bring a bike. For a big public rose garden the same month, [[keisei-rose-garden]] is in Yachiyo, also in Chiba.</p>
`,

'maeda-forest-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria flowers from late May into early June, a month after the wisteria of Honshu has finished. The Fuji Matsuri, the wisteria festival, is held on a Sunday around the end of May. In 2026 it was on 31 May, from 10 am to 3 pm, run by the Sapporo park association and local groups.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The wisteria grows over a long stone pergola, and the purple clusters hang down between the stone pillars. The park is best known for its long, straight canal lined with poplars, leading to a pyramid-shaped viewing platform with a wide view of Sapporo and the Teine mountains.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's in Teine Ward, in the northwest of Sapporo. Buses run from Teine Station on the JR Hakodate Line. For more late spring flowers in Sapporo, the tulips at [[takino-suzuran-hillside-park]] are in flower at the same time.</p>
`,

'northern-culture-museum': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria usually flowers from early to mid May. The museum lights it up for about a week while it's out: in 2026 the light-up ran from 7 to 12 May, from 5:30 to 8 pm, for 500 yen for high school age and up, and free for younger children. Only the courtyard and the second garden are open in the evening, not the house. During the day, entry to the museum is 800 yen for adults.</p>
<h3>The house</h3>
<p>The museum is the former home of the Ito family, once the largest landowners in Niigata, and the house has dozens of rooms around gardens and storehouses. The great wisteria is a single tree, about 150 years old, that covers the whole courtyard trellis. In autumn, the museum lights up its maples too, and in 2026 that's on 14 and 15 November.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 20 minutes by taxi from JR Niigata Station, or 5 minutes by taxi from Niitsu Station on the Shin'etsu Line. Buses from the Bandai City bus centre in Niigata take about 40 minutes to Kami-Sokumi Hakubutsukan-mae. Parking is free, with space for 400 cars. For questions, call the museum on 025-385-2001.</p>
`,

'shoho-ji-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria is usually at its best from early to mid May, later than most wisteria in Kansai. While it's in flower, adults are asked for a 200 yen contribution to its upkeep. For this year's timing, call the Hino tourist association on 0748-52-6577.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>Shoho-ji is a small, quiet hill temple in Kagake, in the countryside of Hino. Its three trellises of old wisteria are said by local people to be more than 300 years old, with clusters up to a metre long. It's the opposite of the famous wisteria gardens: no crowds, no stalls, just the flowers and the temple.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take a bus from Hino Station on the Ohmi Railway to the Kagake stop, then walk about 10 minutes. The buses don't run on Sundays or holidays, so come on a weekday or Saturday, or drive. Hino is an old merchant town, and the Omi merchant houses in the town centre are worth a look on the way back.</p>
`,

'achi-shrine': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria is usually at its best from mid to late April. The shrine holds a wisteria viewing festival, the Fujimi no Kai, on a weekend in late April: in 2026 it was on 25 and 26 April, from 10 am to 3 pm, with music on a stage under the trellis, gagaku court music and a tea ceremony. It's busy over Golden Week.</p>
<h3>The wisteria</h3>
<p>The Achi no Fuji is an Akebono wisteria, a kind with pale pink flowers rather than the usual purple. It's a prefectural natural monument, said to be 300 to 500 years old. It nearly died in 2001, and local people and a tree doctor nursed it back to health.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The shrine sits on Tsurugata Hill, right above the canal quarter of Kurashiki, about 15 minutes' walk from JR Kurashiki Station. Climb the steps from the canal area, and you get a view over the old tiled roofs of the town from the top.</p>
`,

'yuri-no-sato-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Yuri Festa runs through June. In 2026 it was from 31 May to 30 June, with no closing days. Mid June is usually the peak. Entry and parking are free.</p>
<p>Go in the evening if you can. During the festival the lilies are lit up every night from sunset to 9 pm, with projection mapping and lit fountains, and it's cooler.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 150,000 lily flowers of some 20 kinds fill the park, and there's a big play area for children too.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's 20 minutes' walk from Nishi-Nagata Yurinosato Station on the Echizen Railway, or about 20 minutes by car from the Maruoka interchange on the Hokuriku Expressway. Maruoka Castle, one of Japan's twelve original castle keeps, is a short drive away.</p>
`,

'nanrakuen': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The irises are usually best in June. The garden holds flower festivals through the year, including plums in February, satsuki azaleas in late May and the irises in June. In summer it holds a wind chime festival, which ran from 27 June to 23 August in 2026. Entry is 320 yen for adults and 160 yen for school children, and the garden is open from 9 am to 5 pm.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Nanrakuen is the largest Japanese garden in Shikoku, about 15 hectares, laid out in four zones around ponds and streams. About 30,000 irises fill the iris beds, and some 36,000 satsuki azaleas flower just before them. It's a modern garden, opened in 1985, so the paths are wide and easy to walk.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 5 minutes by car from the Tsushima-Iwamatsu interchange on the Uwajima Road, with free parking for 530 cars. Buses run from Uwajima Station. For questions, call the garden on 0895-32-3344.</p>
`,

'suigo-itako-ayame-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Suigo Itako Iris Festival runs from late May to late June. In 2026 it was from 22 May to 21 June, and it was the 75th. The irises are usually best around 10 June. Entry to the garden is free and it's open all day.</p>
<h3>The bridal boat</h3>
<p>The highlight is the yomeiri-bune, a re-creation of the old way a bride travelled to her new home in Itako, standing in a hand-rowed boat along the canal. It runs on set days and times during the festival, a few times a week, so check the timetable first. You can also take a rowed boat ride through the irises yourself; in past years it cost 1,000 yen for adults.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The garden is next to the canal, a short walk from JR Itako Station on the Kashima Line. Highway buses also run from Tokyo Station to Itako in about an hour and a quarter. For questions during the festival, call the festival office on 0299-63-1187.</p>
`,

'motsu-ji-temple-iris-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The iris festival runs through the second half of June. In 2026 it was from 10 to 30 June, and the irises are usually at their best in the last ten days of the month. At weekends during the festival, there are performances of the Ennen no Mai, an old temple dance, and a children's drawing contest.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 30,000 irises of some 300 kinds grow in a garden of about 3,000 square metres by the Oizumi-ga-ike pond. Motsu-ji is part of the Hiraizumi World Heritage site, and its pond garden is one of the best surviving examples of a Pure Land garden, laid out in the 12th century to represent the Buddhist paradise. The irises were planted from 1953.</p>
<p>Entry to the temple is 700 yen for adults, 400 yen for high school students and 200 yen for primary and junior high school children.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a 10 minute walk from JR Hiraizumi Station. Chuson-ji, with its gold-covered Konjikido hall, is a short bus ride away. For questions, call the temple on 0191-46-2331.</p>
`,

'kasui-yuri-no-en': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The garden opens only in lily season, from late May to early July. In 2026 it was open from 23 May to 5 July, from 9 am to 5 pm. Peak is usually June, and the fee goes up then: in 2026 adults paid 1,100 yen in the quieter weeks and 1,500 yen from 30 May to 25 June.</p>
<h3>The lilies</h3>
<p>About 2 million lilies of some 150 kinds fill 15 hectares beside Kasui-sai temple. The Asiatic lilies flower first, in late May and early June, then the big scented hybrid lilies take over. It's one of the biggest lily gardens in Japan.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take a bus from JR Fukuroi Station to the Kasui stop, about 15 minutes, then walk 5 minutes. By car, it's about 3 km from the Fukuroi interchange on the Tomei Expressway. Parking is paid, with 300 spaces and a 500-space overflow car park at peak. The roads get jammed at peak weekends, so the garden's website suggests routes. Kasui-sai temple next door is worth a look too, and it holds a peony festival in spring. For questions, call 0538-43-4736.</p>
`,

'koiwa-iris-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Early to mid June. The Koiwa Iris Garden Festival ran from 30 May to 14 June in 2026, and on 28 May the flowers were about 60 per cent open, so the first week of June is a good bet. Entry is free.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 50,000 irises of some 100 kinds grow in beds on the bank of the Edo River, with the wide river and the Chiba shore beyond. It's a simple, open garden, and on a weekday it's peaceful. The banks of the Edo River are good for a walk afterwards.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>During the festival, direct buses run at weekends from the south exit of JR Koiwa Station, stop 2. On festival weekends a free shuttle also runs to Shobunuma Park in Adachi Ward, another iris garden, about 30 minutes away. Tokyo's best-known iris garden, [[horikiri-iris-garden]] in Katsushika, is close by too, so you could see three in a day.</p>
`,

'kitayama-park-iris-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Higashimurayama Iris Festival is held every year from early to mid June, while the irises are out. In 2025 it ran from 31 May to 15 June. Mid June is usually the peak. The park is free.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 8,000 clumps, some 100,000 flowers of around 600 kinds, fill a garden laid out like old rice paddies, with wooden walkways between the beds. Behind it rises the wooded hill of Hachikokuyama, so it feels like the countryside, though you're in the suburbs of Tokyo. The hill is the setting thought to have inspired the woods in the film <em>My Neighbor Totoro</em>, and it's a pleasant walk.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 15 minutes' walk from Higashimurayama Station or Seibuen Station on the Seibu lines. There's no parking, so come by train. For questions, call the park on 042-394-1988.</p>
`,

'kamo-shobuen': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The iris festival runs from late May to early June. In 2026 it was from 23 May to 7 June, with the beds lit up in the evening from sunset to 9 pm on some days. Entry is free, and parking is free too.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 37,000 plants of some 300 kinds, from all three main groups of Japanese iris: Edo, Higo and Ise. The local community tends the garden, and that's part of its appeal. The Ise irises, with their soft drooping petals, are less common than the other two, so look out for them.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The garden is in the Kamo district in the north of Toyohashi. For this year's dates and hours, call the festival committee at Toyohashi City Hall on 0532-51-2430. Toyohashi's other iris garden, at Akatsukayama Park, holds its own iris festival at the same time.</p>
`,

'southeast-botanical-gardens': `
<h3>When to go now</h3>
<p>The Okinawa Nangoku Illumination is on right now. The 2026 to 2027 season runs from 23 October 2026 to 23 May 2027, every evening from 5 to 10 pm, with last entry at 9:30. The palms, baobabs and lake are lit up, and it's one of the biggest illuminations in Okinawa. Prices for this season hadn't been confirmed when we checked, and over Christmas and New Year evening-only tickets haven't been sold in past seasons, so check the garden's website before you go.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 1,300 kinds of plants grow across two areas: a water garden around a lake, and a botanical garden with baobabs, palms and other tropical trees. The palm avenue reflected in the lake is the classic photo. In early summer the lotus pond flowers too.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car or a tour. It's about 5 minutes' drive from the Okinawa-Kita interchange on the Okinawa Expressway. Nearby, the [[okinawa-comprehensive-athletic-park-deigo]] has red deigo flowers in spring.</p>
`,

'hunter-mountain-yuri-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lily park opens on the ski slopes in summer, usually from mid July to late August, and the lilies are at their best from late July to mid August. Check this year's dates and fees on the resort's website; in past years, adults paid about 1,000 yen and children about 500 yen, and dogs got in free.</p>
<h3>The lilies</h3>
<p>About 4 million lily flowers of some 50 kinds are planted down the slopes. A flower lift, about 1 km long, carries you over them to the top, and from there walking paths up to about 2 km long wind back down through the lilies. Being up in the mountains, it's cooler than the plains in midsummer.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Hunter Mountain Shiobara is about an hour by taxi from Nasushiobara Station on the Tohoku Shinkansen, so most people drive. Shiobara Onsen, one of the best-known hot spring towns in Tochigi, is on the way, and a night there makes a good weekend.</p>
`,

'toshodai-ji-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lotus usually flowers from late June to early August, and July is the best month. Go early: lotus flowers open in the morning and close by early afternoon. The temple opens at 8:30 am. Toshodai-ji is part of the Nara Nishinokyo Lotus Road, which links four temples with lotus collections. In 2026 it ran from 18 June to 2 August, with a combined ticket for all four temples at 4,000 yen, including a gift.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>Toshodai-ji was founded in 759 by Ganjin, the Chinese monk who crossed to Japan after five failed attempts and went blind on the way. Its Golden Hall is one of the finest buildings of the Nara period, and the temple is part of the Nara World Heritage site. The lotus grows in big pots and in ponds around the grounds, and some kinds are linked to Ganjin.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a 10 minute walk from Nishinokyo Station on the Kintetsu Kashihara Line. Yakushi-ji, another of the Lotus Road temples, is right next to the station, so it's easy to see both. For lotus details, the Nara city tourist association is on 0742-30-0230.</p>
`,

'nanko-sunflower-fields': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Nanko Sunflower Festival runs from mid July to early August. In 2026 it was from 18 July to 2 August, and it ended with a fireworks show of about 600 rockets on the last night. The sunflowers are planted in three venues that flower one after another, so something is at its peak through most of the festival. Entry to the fields was 300 yen for junior high age and up and 100 yen for primary school children.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 560,000 sunflowers on some 11 hectares across three farming villages, among the biggest sunflower fields in western Japan. The fields are in the hills of western Hyogo, so the sunflowers stand against green mountains rather than a flat plain.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>On weekends and holidays during the festival, a free shuttle bus runs between Harima-Tokusa Station on the JR Kishin Line and the festival site. With a car, it's easier to visit all three venues. For questions, call Sayo Town on 0790-82-0670.</p>
`,

'jupia-land-hirata': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The World Hydrangea and Lily Festival runs from late June to late July. In 2026 it was from 20 June to 20 July, and early July is usually the peak. Fees have changed over the years, from 500 yen in 2023 to 800 yen for high school age and up in a later listing, and junior high age and under get in free.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 28,000 hydrangeas of some 825 kinds cover the hillside, including many rare and foreign kinds you won't see in temple gardens. In 2018 it set a record for the most hydrangea varieties on display, at 372. The village celebrates 14 July as its hydrangea day.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Hirata is in the hills of central Fukushima, and you'll need a car. In May, the same park holds a moss phlox festival, see [[jupia-land-hirata-may]]. For questions, call the park office on 0247-55-3535.</p>
`,

'hana-hasu-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lotus festival runs from late June to early August. In 2026 the 34th festival ran from 27 June to 9 August. July is the best month, and mornings are best, because lotus flowers open early and close by the afternoon.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 130 kinds of lotus from around the world grow in the ponds, in different sizes and colours. Seeing the different kinds side by side is the point. If you only know the standard pink lotus, it's an eye-opener.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The park is in Minamiechizen, in southern Fukui. It's easiest by car from the Hokuriku Expressway. For more summer flowers in Fukui, the lilies of [[yuri-no-sato-park]] finish just before the lotus starts.</p>
`,

'yakushi-ike-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lotus is usually at its best in July, often the middle of the month. Go early in the morning, when the flowers open. The park is free.</p>
<p>The same park has a full calendar. Before the lotus, the iris and hydrangea festival runs through June: in 2026 it was from 30 May to 28 June. The pond is also a cherry spot in spring.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Yakushi-ike is a pond park in the hills of Machida, in western Tokyo, now part of the Machida Yakushi-ike Park Shikisai no Mori. It has an old farmhouse, a small temple on the hill and woodland walks, so it makes a gentle half day.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from Machida Station to the park. For other lotus in Tokyo, [[shinobazu-pond]] in Ueno is easier to reach, and it flowers at the same time.</p>
`,

'tokai-university-sapporo-lavender-field': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lavender is usually at its best in July, and the Minamisawa Lavender Festival is held on a day in mid July. In 2026 it was the 17th, on 11 July, from 10 am to 3 pm, with a lavender concert by the university brass band, live music, stalls and workshops. It's free, and it can be cancelled in bad weather.</p>
<h3>Why here</h3>
<p>The Minamisawa district of Sapporo, where the campus stands, is said to be where commercial lavender growing in Japan began. The university keeps the lavender field going on its campus, and the festival is a friendly local event rather than a tourist sight, which is its charm.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take a Jotetsu bus from Makomanai Station on the Namboku subway line to Tokai Daigaku-mae. You can't drive onto the campus. For the big lavender fields, [[farm-tomita]] in Nakafurano is about two hours away by car. For questions, call the campus on 011-571-5111.</p>
`,

'itabashi-tropical-botanical-garden': `
<h3>Why go</h3>
<p>It's a small, inexpensive glasshouse that recreates a tropical forest, and it's a good escape from the Tokyo heat or rain. The most unusual part is a cool room that recreates a tropical cloud forest, the misty forest found high on tropical mountains, where wild begonias grow among ferns and mossy rocks. It's a rare chance to see begonias as they grow in the wild, rather than in pots. There's also an aquarium section with fish from tropical waters.</p>
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>In 2024, adults paid 260 yen, and children and over-65s 130 yen. Primary and junior high school children got in free at weekends, and preschoolers were always free. It's closed on Mondays, or the next day when Monday is a holiday. Check the website for current fees before you go.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a short walk from Takashimadaira Station on the Toei Mita Line. You'll need an hour at most. For a bigger begonia display in Tokyo, the [[jindai-botanical-gardens-begonia-room]] in Chofu is in flower all year.</p>
`,

'shuri-sagaribana-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Sagaribana usually flower in Okinawa from June to August, with late June and early July the most reliable. In 2026, flowers were already opening around Naha by mid June. The flowers open in the evening and drop by morning, so come after dark, from about 8 pm. Local groups have held evening viewing events on Baba-dori in early July in past years.</p>
<h3>The flowers</h3>
<p>Sagaribana hang in long strings like bunches of grapes, and each flower opens as a soft white or pale pink puff, with a sweet scent that you'll notice before you see them. By dawn most have fallen. About 30 trees line some 150 m of Baba-dori, in the quiet residential streets of Shuri Sakiyama-cho, and the street is lit up in season. Please keep your voice down: people live here.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes' walk from Shuri Station on the Yui Rail. Shuri Castle is close by, so you can visit the castle in the afternoon and walk over after dark. For the most famous sagaribana in Japan, see [[iriomote-sagaribana]].</p>
`,

'iriomote-sagaribana': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The sagaribana usually flower from late June to mid July, and tour companies run trips from mid June to the end of July. The flowers open at night and fall into the river by dawn, so the classic way to see them is an early-morning canoe or kayak tour, setting off in the dark and paddling into the mangroves as the sky gets light. You'll see the flowers still hanging from the trees, and fallen ones floating on the water.</p>
<h3>Booking a tour</h3>
<p>Tours usually last about three and a half hours and include pick-up from hotels in the Uehara area. Book well ahead: it's a short season and the tours are popular. Please don't go into the mangroves alone in the dark. The rivers are tidal, and the forest is easy to get lost in.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Iriomote is reached by ferry from Ishigaki, about 40 to 50 minutes. Most of the island is jungle and a national park, and it's part of a World Natural Heritage site. If you can't make it to Iriomote, the [[shuri-sagaribana-avenue]] in Naha is the easiest place to see the flowers.</p>
`,

'shikisai-no-oka': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The flower stripes are at their fullest from mid July to mid September, with the most colour from late July to August. From July to September, entry is 800 yen for adults (16 and over) and 500 yen for children aged 7 to 15. The rest of the year it's open with a smaller display. In summer it's open from 8:40 am to 5:30 pm.</p>
<h3>The hill</h3>
<p>About 15 kinds of flowers, around 350,000 plants, are planted in long stripes across 14 hectares of rolling hillside, with the Tokachi mountains behind. It's one of the most photographed views in Hokkaido. To see more of it, ride the tractor-drawn Norokko bus (15 minutes, 500 yen for high school age and up, 300 yen for children), or hire a cart. There's also an alpaca farm.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 12 minutes' walk from Bibaushi Station on the JR Furano Line, mostly uphill. Note that the Furano-Biei Norokko sightseeing train, which used to run on this line every summer, made its last runs in 2026. [[farm-tomita]], the most famous lavender farm, is about 30 minutes south. In June, the [[shikisai-no-oka-june|same hill]] has its early summer flowers.</p>
`,

'tsunan-sunflower-square': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The sunflower square opens from late July to mid August. In 2026 it was from 24 July to 16 August, from 9 am to 5 pm. The sunflowers are planted in three blocks that flower one after another, so something is at its peak through most of the season.</p>
<h3>The square</h3>
<p>About 500,000 sunflowers cover four hectares in the hills of southern Niigata. There's a sunflower maze, which children love, and a viewing platform for the full view. Entry is free.</p>
<p>Parking costs 1,000 yen per car, and it comes with a 300 yen coupon to spend at the stalls or shops in town. There's space for about 180 cars.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>There are no buses, so take a taxi from Tsunan Station on the JR Iiyama Line, about 15 minutes. The area is part of the Echigo-Tsumari Art Field, home of the Echigo-Tsumari Art Triennale, so you can combine the sunflowers with outdoor art in the surrounding villages.</p>
`,

'sarukubo-rice-field-park-sunflowers': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The sunflowers are sown to peak around the Obon holiday in mid August, and the season is short, about a week either side. In 2026 a visitor on 17 August found them at their peak. Go in the morning, when the flowers face the sun and the light is softer.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 200,000 sunflowers are planted in fallow rice fields around the Sarukubo Rice Field Park, beside the JR Karasuyama Line between Kobana and Taki stations. The planted area has grown over the years: in 2024 it was 1.4 hectares. The classic photo is the little train passing the sunflowers, and in peak years JR has run special trains that slow down beside the fields.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Kobana Station is about 12 minutes' walk away. The Karasuyama Line runs from Hoshakuji, north of Utsunomiya, with battery-powered ACCUM trains. It's a quiet country line, so check the timetable: trains are about once an hour.</p>
`,

'izunuma-and-uchinuma': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lotus festival runs from late July to late August. In 2026 it was from 25 July to 30 August, from 8 am to 4 pm, with three boat landings at Hasama, Wakayanagi and Tsukidate. The lotus opens early in the morning and closes by midday, so go in the morning.</p>
<h3>The boats</h3>
<p>Small boats carry you through the lotus for about 25 minutes, with separate boats on Izunuma and Uchinuma. In 2026 the fare was 1,500 yen for adults and 500 yen for children. Boats can stop in bad weather or at low water, so check before you go: the Kurihara tourist association is on 0228-25-4166.</p>
<h3>The lakes</h3>
<p>Izunuma and Uchinuma are protected wetlands, listed under the Ramsar Convention, and in winter they're one of Japan's most important sites for migrating geese and swans. In summer the lotus covers large parts of both lakes. You'll need a car. It's about 20 minutes from Nitta Station on the JR Tohoku Line.</p>
`,

'saiho-ji-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The spider lilies are usually at their best in the second half of September, sometimes into early October. In 2026 they were about half open on 14 September and expected to peak that weekend, so mid to late September is the time to aim for. The temple sometimes lights them up in the evening, depending on the flowers, and announces it on social media.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>About 20,000 spider lilies line both sides of the approach to Saiho-ji's thatched gate, and they're not only red: there are yellow, white and even pink ones among them. The old thatched roof with red flowers below is the photo everyone comes for. It's a working temple, so be quiet and keep to the paths.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 5 minutes' walk from Nippa Station on the Yokohama Blue Line, so it's an easy trip from central Yokohama or Shin-Yokohama. For the biggest spider lily field near Tokyo, see [[kinchakuda-manjushage-park]] in Saitama.</p>
`,

'mishima-taisha-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree flowers twice a year: first in early to mid September, then again from late September into early October. The scent is the thing, so you'll often notice it before you reach the tree. Each flowering lasts only a few days, so if you're coming from far away, check the shrine's social media for news before you go. Entry is free.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The great osmanthus is put at over 1,200 years old and is a national natural monument. It's an usugi-mokusei, with pale yellow flowers, rather than the orange kinmokusei you'll smell all over Japan in October.</p>
<h3>The shrine</h3>
<p>Mishima Taisha is the chief shrine of the old province of Izu, and Minamoto no Yoritomo prayed here before he rose against the Taira in 1180. The grounds have big old camphor trees too. It's 15 minutes' walk from Mishima Station, on the Tokaido Shinkansen, so it's an easy stop on the way to or from Izu. Rakujuen garden and the clear spring-fed Genbei River are near the station.</p>
`,

'saga-airport-cosmos-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cosmos are usually at their best from mid October to early November. The fields are planted fresh each year, so the size of the display varies; check with the airport or Saga city before a long trip.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The Saga Airport Cosmos Garden is beside the runway, about a minute by car from the airport terminal. There's nothing tall around it, so the field feels open and wide, and planes pass close by. Time your visit around the arrivals board and you can get a plane and the cosmos in one photo.</p>
<p>On the east side of the airport, the Airport Park has a YS-11 airliner on outdoor display, which plane fans will enjoy.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The airport is south of Saga city, on the shore of the Ariake Sea, and buses run from Saga Station. A car makes it easier. For more cosmos in Kyushu, the [[shirakimine-kogen]] hilltop above Isahaya flowers at the same time, with views over Omura Bay.</p>
`,

'eimei-ji-temple-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October, when kinmokusei flower all over Japan. The orange flowers last only about a week, so the timing is tight. A warm September can push it later. The scent carries across the temple grounds, so you'll know if you've got it right as you arrive.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The tree has been a national natural monument since 1937. Its trunk is about 3.4 m round at eye height, and it splits into two great limbs. It's one of only five kinmokusei in Japan with that status, along with the trees at [[kezo-ji-temple-kinmokusei|Kezo-ji]] in Isesaki, [[mishima-taisha-kinmokusei|Mishima Taisha]], [[oshimori-ji-temple-kinmokusei|Oshimori-ji]] in Ehime and [[asobaru-kinmokusei|Asobaru]] in Kumamoto.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Eimei-ji is in Ora, in the flat farm country of southeast Gunma. You'll need a car or a taxi from the nearest stations on the Tobu Koizumi Line. Kezo-ji's tree is about 40 minutes away, so you can see both national monument trees in one trip.</p>
`,

'kezo-ji-temple-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October. The flowers last only about a week, and the timing moves with the weather, later in a warm year.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>A national natural monument since 1937, the tree has a trunk about 2.6 m round that splits into three main limbs. It's one of only five kinmokusei in Japan with that status. Another, at [[eimei-ji-temple-kinmokusei|Eimei-ji]] in Ora, is in the same prefecture, about 40 minutes away by car.</p>
<h3>Around the temple</h3>
<p>The temple gives its name to the neighbourhood and to Kezoji Park next door, Isesaki's big park, with gardens, a small amusement park and about 1,000 cherry trees that flower in late March and early April. It's about 10 minutes by car from the Isesaki interchange on the Kita-Kanto Expressway.</p>
`,

'asobaru-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Usually late September to early October. Like all kinmokusei, the flowers last only about a week, and a warm autumn makes them late. The scent is said to carry 4 km, so on a still day you'll smell it as you drive through the farms.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The tree is said to be over 770 years old and has been a national natural monument since 1934. Its single trunk, about 2.75 m round, splits into three limbs about 3.5 m up. It stands beside a small Kannon hall in the farmland of Kosa. It's one of only five kinmokusei in Japan with national monument status, and the only one in Kyushu.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car. Kosa is about 40 minutes south of Kumamoto city, in the area hit by the magnitude 7.1 earthquake of 28 July 2026, so check local road conditions before you set out. On the way, [[suizenji-jojuen]] in Kumamoto is one of the finest gardens in Kyushu.</p>
`,

'oshimori-ji-temple-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Usually late September to early October, though a warm autumn can push it later. The flowers last only about a week.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>One of the biggest kinmokusei in Japan, made a national natural monument in 1927, the earliest of the five kinmokusei with that status. It divides into four trunks just above the ground, with roots about 4 m round. Its age isn't known. It's an old tree, and parts of the trunk have rotted hollow, so look without touching.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The temple is in Iioka, in the countryside of Saijo. Buses from Iyo-Saijo Station take about 15 minutes. Saijo is known across Japan for its spring water, which bubbles up from wells all over town, and for its autumn festival in mid October, when dozens of ornate danjiri floats parade through the streets.</p>
`,

'komyo-ji-temple-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October, when the kinmokusei flowers. The scent fills the temple grounds for about a week.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>Komyo-ji is a quiet temple in the pottery town of Mashiko. Its kinmokusei is a town natural monument, and so are the temple's weeping cherry and plum tree, which makes it worth a visit in spring too. The weeping cherry is put at over 180 years old.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>Mashiko is one of Japan's best-known pottery towns, home of the folk-craft potter Hamada Shoji, and the main street is lined with potteries and shops. The Mashiko Pottery Fair is held twice a year, in spring and early November, and it's one of the biggest in Japan. Mashiko is reached by bus from Utsunomiya or by the Moka Railway.</p>
`,

'bios-no-oka-orchids': `
<h3>When to go now</h3>
<p>The autumn orchid festival is on now. In 2026 it runs from 12 September to 30 November, with about 10,000 dendrobium flowers in white, pink and purple hung around the grounds. The park is open from 9 am to 5:30 pm, with last entry at 4:15, and some sources say it closes on Tuesdays, so check the opening calendar on its website. Entry is 2,200 yen for junior high age and up and 1,100 yen for children aged 4 to primary school, including the lake boat ride.</p>
<p>There's a spring orchid festival too, the Urizun festival, so autumn isn't the only chance.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>BIOS no Oka is a subtropical forest park in the hills of Uruma, in central Okinawa. The boat ride across the lake is the highlight, and during the festival the orchids are hung all around the grounds. You can also book a session to make your own dendrobium arrangement, which doesn't need a park ticket. For questions, call 098-965-3400.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car. It's in the middle of the island, about an hour from Naha, so it's easy to combine with a drive to the north. From late October, the [[southeast-botanical-gardens]] in Okinawa City has its big evening illumination, about 20 minutes away.</p>
`,

'okuma-garden-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October, when kinmokusei flower all over Tokyo. The flowers last only about a week. The garden is open on days when the university holds classes, from 9 am to 5 pm from April to September and 9 am to 4:30 pm from October to March, and it's closed in bad weather. Check Waseda's website for the open days before you go. It's free, and you don't need to be a student.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The garden belonged to Okuma Shigenobu, the statesman who founded Waseda University and twice served as prime minister. It's a lawn-and-pond garden in the Japanese style, right next to the main campus, with the Okuma Auditorium clock tower rising behind it. The big kinmokusei draws white-eyes and tits when it flowers.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 5 minutes' walk from Waseda Station on the Tozai Line. For more kinmokusei in Tokyo, [[hikarigaoka-park-kinmokusei|Hikarigaoka Park]] has a whole avenue of them.</p>
`,

'hikarigaoka-park-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The scent usually peaks around the start of October, and it lasts only about a week. Kinmokusei flower all at once across Tokyo, so when you start to smell them in your neighbourhood, go that weekend. A warm September pushes them later. The park is free and open all day.</p>
<h3>Where to look</h3>
<p>About 50 kinmokusei line a path of about 300 m along the south side of the park's bird sanctuary, so you walk through the scent rather than past a single tree. Three tall old trees stand in Yurinoki Plaza too. It's one of the best places in Tokyo to see, and smell, kinmokusei in quantity.</p>
<h3>Come back in November</h3>
<p>Hikarigaoka is a big park in Nerima, on the site of a former airfield, with wide lawns and sports grounds. In late November, its [[hikarigaoka-park-ginkgo-avenue|ginkgo avenue]] turns gold. It's 8 minutes' walk from Hikarigaoka Station, the end of the Oedo Line.</p>
`,

'jindai-botanical-gardens-osmanthus': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October. The three kinds of osmanthus flower at about the same time, and each lasts only about a week, so for the best chance of seeing all three, go around the start of October.</p>
<h3>Three kinds side by side</h3>
<p>The garden grows all three: the orange kinmokusei, the white ginmokusei and the pale yellow usugi-mokusei, in several places, including around the azalea garden. The kinmokusei has the strongest scent, the one that fills Tokyo every October. The ginmokusei is fainter and sweeter. It's the easiest place in Tokyo to compare them.</p>
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>Adults pay 500 yen, and the garden is closed on Mondays, or the next day when Monday is a holiday. It's also the season of the autumn rose festival, so you can see both on the same visit; see [[jindai-botanical-gardens]]. Jindai-ji temple and its soba shops are right next door. Buses run from Chofu Station on the Keio Line.</p>
`,

'hibiya-park-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October. The flowers last only about a week, so when you start smelling kinmokusei around the city, go that week. The park is free and open all day.</p>
<h3>Where to look</h3>
<p>Kinmokusei grow throughout the park, with a group near the Iwaidaimon gate on the Imperial Palace side. White ginmokusei flower alongside them, with a softer scent. It's an easy detour between the palace and Ginza, and the scent is strongest on a still, warm afternoon.</p>
<h3>Other seasons</h3>
<p>Hibiya Park opened in 1903 as Tokyo's first Western-style park. In spring it has [[hibiya-park|nemophila and tulips]], and in late November the old [[hibiya-park-kubikake-ginkgo|Kubikake ginkgo]] turns gold. It's 2 minutes' walk from Hibiya Station.</p>
`,

'kitanomaru-park-kinmokusei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>Late September to early October, when the kinmokusei flower. Their scent drifts across the lawns along with flowering abelia. The park is free and open all day.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Kitanomaru Park covers the northern grounds of old Edo Castle, next to the Imperial Palace. It's home to the Nippon Budokan and the Science Museum, and it has wide lawns and woods, so it's a calm place to walk among the old castle walls. The National Museum of Modern Art is nearby.</p>
<h3>Other seasons</h3>
<p>In late March, the moat beside the park becomes [[chidorigafuchi]], one of Tokyo's most famous cherry walks. It's 5 minutes' walk from Kudanshita Station. In autumn, you can also walk on to [[yasukuni-shrine-ginkgo|Yasukuni Shrine]], whose ginkgos turn gold in late November.</p>
`,

'meiji-jingu-chrysanthemum-show': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The show runs from late October to late November, timed with the shrine's Autumn Grand Festival around 3 November, the birthday of the Emperor Meiji. In 2024 it ran from 25 October to 23 November, and the dates are similar each year. The flowers are freshest in the first half of November. It's free.</p>
<h3>What you'll see</h3>
<p>Prize chrysanthemums line the main approach near the inner shrine, grown by enthusiasts and offered to the shrine. Look out for styles such as <em>kengai</em>, plants trained over months to cascade like a waterfall down a cliff, and the <em>ozukuri</em>, a single plant trained to carry hundreds of flowers in a perfect dome. The chrysanthemum is the imperial crest, so it has a special place at Meiji Jingu.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 5 minutes' walk from Harajuku Station to the south gate, then about 10 minutes along the forest path to the inner shrine. In late November, the [[meiji-jingu-gaien-ginkgo-avenue|Gaien ginkgo avenue]], on the shrine's outer grounds, turns gold, and in June the [[meiji-jingu-inner-garden|inner garden]] has its irises.</p>
`,

'keisei-rose-garden-october': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The autumn rose season runs from mid October to late November, and the colours deepen as the nights get colder, so late October and early November are often the best. The 2026 autumn event runs from 10 October to 23 November, from 9 am to 5 pm, with some early mornings and evening openings on set days.</p>
<p>Adults pay 1,700 yen and primary and junior high school children 800 yen. Advance tickets online are a little cheaper.</p>
<h3>Why go in autumn</h3>
<p>Autumn roses are fewer and smaller than in spring, but the colours are richer and the scent stronger, because the flowers open slowly in the cool air. About 1,000 kinds and 7,000 plants flower in autumn, out of some 1,600 kinds in the garden, one of the biggest rose collections in the Kanto region. It's also much quieter than in May.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 15 minutes' walk from Yachiyo-Midorigaoka Station on the Toyo Rapid Railway, which connects with the Tozai Line. Parking is paid. For the spring season, see [[keisei-rose-garden]].</p>
`,

'gyoko-dori-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn gold from late November to early December. A sunny afternoon is best, when the light falls across the trees and the red-brick Tokyo Station building behind them. It's free and open all day.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>Gyoko-dori runs from the Marunouchi side of Tokyo Station towards the Imperial Palace, about 73 m wide and paved in stone, with a pedestrian promenade down the middle and ginkgos on either side. It was rebuilt around 2010, along with the restoration of the station building and its square, and some of the ginkgos that had been lost were replanted. The road is used for state occasions, such as new ambassadors riding to the palace in a horse-drawn carriage.</p>
<h3>Make it a walk</h3>
<p>Gyoko-dori takes 10 minutes at most, so turn it into a walk. Continue to the palace moat and the Imperial Palace East Gardens, or walk south through Marunouchi to [[hibiya-park]], where the old [[hibiya-park-kubikake-ginkgo|Kubikake ginkgo]] turns at the same time.</p>
`,

'meiji-jingu-gaien-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The trees usually start turning in mid November and are fully gold in late November, often into early December. The Jingu Gaien Ginkgo Festival, with food stalls, runs from mid November to early December. The 2026 dates hadn't been announced when we checked. The avenue has been lit up in the evenings in late November in past years.</p>
<p>Go early on a weekday morning. On weekend afternoons at peak, the avenue is shoulder to shoulder.</p>
<h3>Things to know for 2026</h3>
<p>The domed Meiji Memorial Picture Gallery at the end of the avenue, the backdrop of the classic photo, is under construction in 2026, with scaffolding in view. The Christmas market that used to run alongside the ginkgos isn't being held at Gaien this year. The trees themselves are as good as ever.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>146 ginkgos in four rows line about 300 m from Aoyama-dori, pruned into neat cones and planted in order of height, tallest at the Aoyama end, so the avenue looks longer than it is. It's a short walk from Aoyama-itchome or Gaienmae stations. The [[meiji-jingu-chrysanthemum-show]] at the inner shrine runs at the same time.</p>
`,

'showa-kinen-park-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Autumn Leaves Festival and Autumn Night Walk runs from 29 October to 29 November in 2026, and the ginkgos are usually at their best from mid to late November. The park is open until 9 pm during the festival, with the ginkgo avenue, the canal and the Japanese garden lit up from 4:30 to 8:30 pm. The night walk has been named a Night View Heritage site.</p>
<p>Entry is 450 yen for adults and 210 yen for over-65s, and junior high age and under get in free. After 31 October, the evening Japanese garden needs a separate ticket, from 1,200 yen for adults.</p>
<h3>The avenues</h3>
<p>There are two. One runs beside the long canal fountains from the Tachikawa gate, and the other, the Katarai ginkgo avenue, is near Lake Kanko. The fallen leaves cover the ground so thickly that the paths turn solid gold, and you're allowed to walk through them. The park is huge, so hire a bike at the gate to get around.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The Nishi-Tachikawa gate is 2 minutes' walk from Nishi-Tachikawa Station on the JR Ome Line. The same park has a big cosmos display in autumn, see [[showa-kinen-park]], and tulips in April.</p>
`,

'korankei': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak in the second half of November. The Korankei Momiji Festival runs through the season, and the gorge is lit up every evening until 9 pm. Sources give different dates for 2026, so check with the Asuke tourist association before you go. Kyoto's leaves are forecast to be late this year, and Korankei may follow.</p>
<h3>Beat the traffic</h3>
<p>Korankei is famous for its traffic jams. At peak weekends the roads into Asuke back up for hours, and parking costs around 1,000 yen in November. Go on a weekday, arrive early, or skip the car: in the leaf season, direct buses run from Yakusa Station on the Aichi Loop Line. The Tokai-Kanjo Expressway now only lets ETC-equipped cars on and off, so without ETC, use the Toyota interchange on the Tomei.</p>
<h3>The gorge</h3>
<p>About 4,000 maples line the Tomoe River. The most famous view is the red Taigetsu bridge with the maples on the slope behind. Climb up to Kojaku-ji, the temple whose monk is said to have planted the first maples, and on weekend evenings the temple has bamboo lanterns. The old merchant street of Asuke, a short walk away, has good places for lunch.</p>
`,

'kyoto-gyoen-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn in the second half of November, and the leaves stay on the ground into early December. In 2026, Kyoto's autumn leaves are forecast to be later than usual, so the end of November is a safer bet. It's free and open all day.</p>
<h3>Where to look</h3>
<p>Kyoto Gyoen is the big park around the Imperial Palace, and the ginkgos are scattered across it rather than in one place. Around the site of the old Gyokado there are about 20 big ginkgos, and the ground beneath them turns yellow. The ginkgo avenue near the Sawaragi gate is low enough to see the leaves at eye level. The site of the old Ichijo residence has more.</p>
<h3>Why go</h3>
<p>Kyoto in late November is crowded, and the temples with the famous maples can be packed. The Gyoen is huge and open, so it's one of the calmest places in the city to see autumn colour. It's 5 minutes' walk from Marutamachi or Imadegawa stations on the Karasuma Line. For maples, [[tofuku-ji-temple]] and [[eikando-temple]] peak at the same time.</p>
`,

'midosuji-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into early December, a little later than in Tokyo. The 2026 autumn is forecast to be warmer than usual, so the leaves may be late. The street is free and open all day.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>Midosuji is Osaka's main boulevard, about 4 km from Umeda to Namba, lined with around 970 ginkgos on both sides. They were planted when the road was widened in the 1930s. You don't need to walk all of it: pick a stretch between two subway stations, such as Yodoyabashi to Hommachi through the business district, or on to Shinsaibashi for the shops.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Osaka's other big ginkgo spot is [[osaka-castle-park-ginkgo|Osaka Castle Park]], whose ginkgos turn at the same time. The Midosuji subway line runs right under the avenue, so you can walk a section and hop back on.</p>
`,

'university-of-tokyo-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November to early December, and in some years they've peaked as late as the first week of December. The campus is open to visitors, and it's free to walk.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>The avenue runs from the main gate on Hongo-dori to the Yasuda Auditorium, the brick tower that's the symbol of the university, and the tall ginkgos meet overhead. The ginkgo leaf is the university's emblem. The campus is a working university, so be quiet near the buildings, and don't go into lecture halls.</p>
<p>The university holds its Homecoming Day, with stalls and a music festival along the ginkgo avenue, in mid October, before the leaves turn. In 2026 it was on 17 October.</p>
<h3>Around the campus</h3>
<p>Look for Sanshiro Pond, a quiet pond garden from the Edo period, named after a novel by Natsume Soseki. The famous red gate, Akamon, is a few minutes' walk south. It's 5 minutes' walk from Todaimae Station on the Namboku Line, or 10 minutes from Hongo-sanchome.</p>
`,

'tofuku-ji-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak in the second half of November, but 2026 is forecast to be late, with one forecast putting Kyoto's maples at their best around 11 December. The temple's autumn special opening runs from 14 November to 6 December in 2026, with the Tsutenkyo bridge and the Hojo garden open 30 minutes earlier than usual. Last entry is at 4 pm, and the gates close at 4:30.</p>
<p>In autumn, the Tsutenkyo bridge costs 1,000 yen for adults and 500 yen for children, and the Hojo garden 600 yen for adults and 300 yen for children. There's no combined ticket in the autumn season.</p>
<h3>Beat the crowds</h3>
<p>Tofuku-ji is one of the busiest autumn spots in Kyoto, and photos are banned on the Tsutenkyo bridge in the leaf season to keep the crowds moving. Get there for the opening on a weekday. In some years the temple has offered early-morning private viewings, booked through its website, so check.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>About 2,000 maples fill the Sengyokukan ravine, and from the bridge they look like a sea of red. The Hojo garden, by Shigemori Mirei, has a famous chequerboard of moss and stone. It's 10 minutes' walk from Tofukuji Station on the JR Nara Line or the Keihan line, and one stop from Fushimi Inari.</p>
`,

'igano-flower-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The kochia usually start turning red in early October and are at their reddest around mid October. Go then for the deep red, or in late September for the soft green bushes just starting to blush. Entry and parking are free.</p>
<p>Kochia dry out and turn a golden brown in late autumn, and they're traditionally used to make brooms. In 2026 the garden held a free broom-making workshop on 1 November with the old plants.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The Igano flower garden is a hillside kochia field in Haba, in the Tsukiyono area of Minakami, with a gazebo at the top looking out over the rounded bushes and the mountains of northern Gunma. It's a small, local garden, much quieter than the big kochia displays at [[hitachi-seaside-park-october|Hitachi Seaside Park]]. For questions, call the garden on 090-2674-2177.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car. Minakami is a hot spring and outdoor sports area, and the kochia season overlaps with the autumn leaves in the mountains around it. Tanbara, near Numata, has another kochia garden, see [[tambara-lavender-park]].</p>
`,

'fukuyama-rose-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>There are two seasons. Spring is the big one: the roses peak in mid to late May, and the Fukuyama Rose Festival is held on a weekend in mid May. In 2026 it was the 59th, on 16 and 17 May, and the roses are usually best for about two weeks either side. The autumn roses flower from mid October into early December, with fewer flowers but deeper colours and a quieter park. It's free.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The park reopened in April 2024 after renovation, with about 7,000 roses of some 670 kinds on 15,000 square metres, laid out in themed areas. Older guides quote smaller numbers from before the rebuild.</p>
<p>Fukuyama calls itself the City of Roses. After the city was burned in the war, residents planted roses to bring colour back, and there are now roses all over town, along the streets and in front of homes.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from Fukuyama Station, on the Sanyo Shinkansen. Fukuyama Castle is right next to the station. Tomonoura, an old port town with views over the Seto Inland Sea, is about 30 minutes away by bus.</p>
`,

'shima-city-tourist-farm': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The farm opens its kochia field on 1 October. The bushes are green until about mid October, then turn red through late October and early November. The cosmos field opened on 29 October in 2026 and runs until 23 November, from 9 am to 4 pm, so from late October you can see the red kochia and the cosmos together.</p>
<p>Entry is 300 yen, and junior high age and under get in free. Parking is free, and dogs on leads are welcome.</p>
<h3>The farm</h3>
<p>The farm is run by Shima City behind the Ise-Shima roadside station on Route 167. It changes with the seasons: nemophila and moss phlox in spring, then kochia and cosmos in autumn. It's a simple farm field rather than a polished park, but the colours are bright and it's never as crowded as the big flower parks.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>By car, it's about 30 minutes from the Ise-nishi interchange on the Ise Expressway. Ise Grand Shrine is on the way, so the farm makes an easy stop on a day in Ise-Shima. For questions, call Shima City's agriculture section on 0599-44-0288.</p>
`,

'hamamatsu-flower-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The chrysanthemum show is usually held from early to late November. The fee at Hamamatsu Flower Park changes with what's in flower, so check the website for November prices and this year's show dates, or call 053-487-0511.</p>
<h3>What you'll see</h3>
<p>The star of the show is the <em>hyakushu tsugiwake-giku</em>: a single chrysanthemum plant with more than 100 varieties grafted onto it, so one plant carries flowers of every colour and shape. It's an Edo-period craft. The rest of the show has classic exhibition chrysanthemums, and the autumn rose garden flowers at the same time.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Hamamatsu Flower Park sits on the shore of Lake Hamana and is best known for its spring cherries and tulips, see [[hamamatsu-flower-park-april]]. Buses run from Hamamatsu Station, on the Tokaido Shinkansen. The park is in the Kanzanji onsen area on the lake, so you can end the day with a bath.</p>
`,

'tsurugaoka-hachimangu-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into mid December, later than in central Tokyo. The shrine grounds also have maples and other trees that turn at the same time, so the whole place is in autumn colour. It's free.</p>
<h3>The great ginkgo</h3>
<p>For centuries a huge ginkgo stood beside the stone steps to the main hall. Legend says an assassin hid behind it in 1219 to kill the shogun Minamoto no Sanetomo. It fell in a storm in March 2010. The lower trunk was replanted nearby, and new shoots grew from the old roots; one of them is now a young tree, slowly growing where the old one stood. Part of the old trunk is on display in the Kamakura Bunkakan Tsurugaoka Museum café, in the shrine grounds.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes' walk from Kamakura Station along Wakamiya-oji or Komachi-dori. In late November, the maples of [[engakuji-temple]] in Kita-Kamakura are at their best too, and the walk between the two takes about 30 minutes.</p>
`,

'yabakei-gorge': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually turn from late October to mid November, and the inner gorge, Shin-Yabakei, often peaks a little later, into late November. In mid October 2026 the trees were still green. In 2025, the Keisekien garden and the Hisashi maple were lit up from 1 November to 1 December, from 5 to 9 pm. Check this year's dates with the Nakatsu city tourism section (0979-62-9034).</p>
<h3>The views</h3>
<p>Yabakei is a gorge of tall rock pinnacles rising out of maple-covered slopes. The best-known view is Hitome Hakkei, eight views at a glance, a lookout five minutes from the car park where you see eight rock peaks at once. It gets busy at peak weekends, so arrive early.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses from JR Nakatsu Station take about 75 minutes to the inner gorge, but a car is much easier, because the viewpoints are spread out. On the way in, you pass [[aonodomon]], the hand-dug tunnel through the cliffs, whose fields are covered in blue nemophila in spring.</p>
`,

'yoshino-baigo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Yoshino Baigo Plum Festival runs from late February to March. In 2026 it was from 21 February to 22 March, centred on Ome City's Ume no Koen park, and the plums are usually at their best from late February to mid March.</p>
<h3>A plum village coming back</h3>
<p>Yoshino Baigo was once one of the biggest plum spots in the Kanto region, until a plant virus, plum pox, forced all the old trees to be cut down from 2009. Replanting began in 2016, and Ume no Koen now has about 1,200 young trees of some 150 kinds. The trees are still growing, so it doesn't look like the old photos yet, but it gets better every year. It's a rare chance to see a famous plum spot being rebuilt.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Ume no Koen is about 15 minutes' walk from Hinatawada Station on the JR Ome Line, about 90 minutes from Shinjuku. The walk along the Tama River between Hinatawada and Ishigamimae stations is pleasant. For a bigger plum display near Tokyo, try [[ogose-bairin]] in Saitama.</p>
`,

'oishi-park-october': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The kochia are usually at their reddest in mid to late October. In early November they start to fade to a soft pink and then brown. Clear mornings are best, when Mount Fuji stands out across Lake Kawaguchi. The park is free.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Oishi Park is a lakeside flower park on the north shore of Lake Kawaguchi. In autumn, rows of round kochia bushes line the beds with the lake and Fuji behind them, one of the classic Fuji views. In summer, the same park is known for its lavender, see [[oishi-park]]. The park has a café and a shop selling local blueberry jam and lavender goods.</p>
<p>Weekends get busy with tour buses, so go early on a weekday if you can.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take the Kawaguchiko sightseeing bus from Kawaguchiko Station to the Kawaguchiko Natural Living Center stop. In November, the Momiji Corridor on the north shore is the lake's best-known maple spot.</p>
`,

'nishi-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries are usually at their best from late March to early April. In cherry season, cars aren't allowed into the park, so come by subway.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 1,300 cherry trees cover a wooded hill above Hakata Bay. It's the only spot in Fukuoka Prefecture on the list of Japan's 100 best cherry spots. The lookout at the top looks out over the bay to the islands, and there's a shrine, Terumo Shrine, in the middle of the park. Locals come for hanami picnics, and it's busy at weekends.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 15 minutes' walk, partly uphill, from Ohorikoen Station on the Kuko subway line. Ohori Park, with its big lake, is on the way, and the ruins of Fukuoka Castle, another cherry spot, are just beyond it. Don't confuse it with Sendai's Nishi Park, a different cherry spot that flowers in mid April, see [[nishi-park-april]].</p>
`,

'naruko-gorge': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually peak from late October to early November. In the leaf season, from about 10 October to 30 November, the car park by the rest house charges: in recent years it was 500 yen per car. It holds about 250 cars and fills up at peak weekends, so arrive early.</p>
<h3>The view</h3>
<p>The classic view is from the lookout by the Naruko Gorge rest house: the red Ofukazawa bridge spanning the gorge, with the slopes in every shade of red and yellow. The walking path along the gorge is open from 9 am to 4 pm. A train crossing the bridge is the shot photographers wait for, so check the JR Rikuu East Line timetable.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes by taxi from Naruko-Onsen Station on the JR Rikuu East Line. During the leaf season, a temporary bus runs from the station, and National Route 47 can be jammed. Naruko Onsen is one of the best-known hot spring towns in Tohoku, so stay the night and come early. For questions, call the Naruko Onsen tourist association on 0229-83-3441.</p>
`,

'motoara-river-cherry-blossom-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from late March to early April, around the same time as Tokyo. Konosu City publishes the cherry festival dates each spring, and boat rides on the river have been reported at past festivals, so check with the city before planning around one.</p>
<h3>Why we love it</h3>
<p>This is one of our founder Gerald's favourite cherry spots in Japan, and it's the kind of place the site was made for. About 500 somei-yoshino line both banks of the Moto-Arakawa, the old course of the Arakawa River, for some 2.5 km through Fukiage. It's a narrow, quiet river with trees on both sides, and far less crowded than the famous river walks in Tokyo.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes' walk from Fukiage Station on the JR Takasaki Line, about an hour from Ueno. Walk one bank and back along the other. Konosu calls itself a city of flowers, and in October the [[konosu-cosmos-fields]] by the Arakawa are some of the biggest cosmos fields near Tokyo.</p>
`,

'ashikaga-flower-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Great Wisteria Festival runs from mid April to late May. In 2026 it was from 11 April to 20 May, and the great wisteria is usually at its best from late April into early May. The yellow wisteria flowers last, into mid May. The fee changes daily with the flowers, from 900 to 2,300 yen for adults and 500 to 1,200 yen for children in 2026, so peak days cost the most.</p>
<h3>Day or night?</h3>
<p>Both, if you can. The evening light-up is the famous view: the purple wisteria glows over the dark garden, and reflections double it in the ponds. In 2026 the garden stayed open until 8:30 pm during the light-up, and day tickets let you stay on into the evening without paying twice. At peak weekends, arrive early or after 4 pm to avoid the worst of the crowds.</p>
<h3>The wisteria</h3>
<p>The great wisteria is more than 160 years old and covers a trellis of about 1,000 square metres. There's an 80 m tunnel of white wisteria, a yellow wisteria tunnel, and more than 350 wisteria in all. The roses take over from early May to early June.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Ashikaga Flower Park Station on the JR Ryomo Line is 3 minutes' walk from the gate. From Tokyo, it's about two hours by train.</p>
`,

'meguro-river': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak in the last week of March, close to Tokyo's official full bloom date. The Nakameguro cherry festival is held on a weekend in late March: in 2026 it was on 28 and 29 March, from 10 am to 5 pm, centred near Nakameguro Station. The trees are lit with lanterns in the evening while they're in flower.</p>
<h3>How to enjoy it</h3>
<p>Meguro River is a walk, not a picnic spot. There's no lawn, and the narrow paths along the river move slowly at peak. Early morning is the quietest time and the best light. In the evening, the lanterns and the cafés along the river make it lively, but expect shoulder-to-shoulder crowds near Nakameguro Station.</p>
<p>The trees continue for nearly 4 km. If Nakameguro is packed, walk downstream towards Meguro and Gotanda, where it's much quieter.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Nakameguro Station on the Tokyu Toyoko and Hibiya lines is a 5 minute walk away. For a cherry spot where you can sit down, try [[shinjuku-gyoen]] or [[yoyogi-park]].</p>
`,

'kairakuen-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Mito Plum Festival runs from mid February to late March. In 2026 it was the 130th, from 11 February to 22 March. With about 3,000 trees of some 100 kinds, the early plums open in January and the late ones last into March, but late February to early March is usually the peak. On Friday to Sunday evenings during the festival, the garden holds UME The Lights, an illumination from 6 to 8 pm.</p>
<p>Entry to the main garden is 320 yen for adults and 160 yen for children and over-70s. The Kobuntei pavilion costs extra.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Kairakuen is one of Japan's three great gardens, with [[kenrokuen]] and [[okayama-korakuen]]. The Mito lord Tokugawa Nariaki laid it out in 1842 and opened it to the townspeople, which was unusual at the time. Climb up to the Kobuntei, his three-storey pavilion, for the view over the plum groves and Lake Senba.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>During the festival, trains stop at the temporary Kairakuen Station, right by the garden. Otherwise, buses run from Mito Station, about 15 minutes. The Kodokan, Nariaki's old domain school in central Mito, also has plums and is worth adding.</p>
`,

'showa-kinen-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Cosmos Festival runs from mid September to late October. In 2026 it was from 12 September to 25 October. The yellow cosmos on the Flower Hill flower first, in September, and the pink and white Sensation cosmos on the Harappa fields peak in early to mid October.</p>
<p>Entry is 450 yen for adults and 210 yen for over-65s, and junior high age and under get in free. In 2026 the last weekend of the festival was free for everyone.</p>
<h3>The cosmos</h3>
<p>More than 4 million cosmos are planted across several fields, the biggest cosmos display in the Tokyo area. In 2026 the Harappa East field had 200,000 Sensation cosmos, plus red buckwheat flowers for the first time.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The cosmos fields are a long walk from the Tachikawa gate, so use the Nishi-Tachikawa gate, 2 minutes from Nishi-Tachikawa Station on the JR Ome Line, or hire a bike. In November, the park's [[showa-kinen-park-ginkgo-avenue|ginkgo avenues]] turn gold, and in April it has tulips.</p>
`,

'hokkaido-university-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos start turning in mid October and are usually at their best from late October into early November, a month before Tokyo's. The student-run Kin'yosai festival is held at the end of October when the trees peak, and the avenue is lit up in the evening. It's been held since 2011.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>About 70 ginkgos line both sides of a 380 m avenue on the Hokkaido University campus in the middle of Sapporo, and the fallen leaves turn the ground gold. The campus is open to visitors and free, but cars can't come onto the campus. The avenue runs beside a busy road, so stay off the roadway when you take photos.</p>
<h3>Around the campus</h3>
<p>The campus is a big green space a short walk north of Sapporo Station, with old buildings and the bust of William S. Clark, the founding teacher famous for "Boys, be ambitious". The [[hokkaido-university-botanic-garden]], south of the campus, has autumn colour at the same time. It's 4 to 5 minutes' walk from Kita-12-jo Station on the Namboku subway line.</p>
`,

'ikoma-kogen': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cosmos usually flower from mid September through October, with early October the peak. Check this year's festival dates and fee with the Hana no Eki Ikoma Kogen on 0984-27-1919.</p>
<h3>The highland</h3>
<p>Ikoma Kogen is a flower park at about 540 m on a highland in Kobayashi, with the Kirishima mountains behind. About a million cosmos of some 16 kinds fill the slopes in autumn, and in spring it has Iceland poppies and other flowers. The view of the volcanoes over the flowers is the reason to come.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses from JR Kobayashi Station take about 20 minutes, and by car it's about 3 km from the Kobayashi interchange on the Miyazaki Expressway. Ebino Kogen, in the Kirishima mountains, is close by, see [[ebino-kogen-tsutsuji-ga-oka]] for its azaleas in late May.</p>
`,

'kiyozumi-cosmos-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cosmos usually flower from late September to mid October, with early October the peak. In 2026 they were listed from 27 September to 18 October.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 5 million cosmos fill some 7 hectares of fallow rice paddies in Kiyozumi, in the Hikami area of Tamba, near the temple of Tasshin-ji. Local farmers run it, so it's a countryside field rather than a park, with the hills of Tamba around it.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Tasshin-ji, next to the fields, is known for its many old Buddhist statues, and Tamba is famous for its black soybeans and chestnuts, which are in season at the same time. It's easiest by car. For more autumn flowers in Kansai, [[hannya-ji-temple]] in Nara is a cosmos temple.</p>
`,

'takamatsu-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from mid to late April, a little later than central Morioka. The park is part of the Morioka Cherry Festival, held from early to late April. Paper lanterns light the trees from 6 to 9 pm while they're in flower. It's free and open all day.</p>
<h3>The view</h3>
<p>The cherries ring Takamatsu Pond, and the view people come for is the pink trees reflected in the water with snow-capped Mount Iwate behind. Sources put the number of trees anywhere from 800 to 1,500. It's on the list of Japan's 100 best cherry spots.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Takamatsu Park is in the north of Morioka. Buses run from Morioka Station. In the same weeks, [[morioka-castle-ruins-park]] in the city centre has more cherries, and the famous rock-splitting cherry, [[ishiwarizakura]], is near the courthouse.</p>
`,

'sera-kogen-farm': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The sunflower festival runs from late July to late August. In 2026 it was from 25 July to 23 August, from 9 am to 5 pm, with longer hours over Obon. The sunflowers are planted in sections that flower in turn, so something is at its peak through the whole festival. The fee changes with how much is in flower: in 2026 adults paid 700 to 1,200 yen and children aged 4 to primary school 300 to 600 yen.</p>
<h3>The fields</h3>
<p>About 6.5 hectares of sunflowers cover the hilltop farm, including white sunflowers and branching kinds that carry up to 100 flowers on one plant. There's a sunflower maze with a quiz for children.</p>
<h3>Other seasons</h3>
<p>The farm opens for different flowers through the year: [[sera-kogen-farm-april|tulips]] in spring, sunflowers in summer, and dahlias in autumn. Its sister garden, [[flower-village-kamu-no-sato]], is nearby. You'll need a car.</p>
`,

'kawazu-river': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Kawazu Cherry Blossom Festival usually starts around the beginning of February and runs into early March, and it's often extended if the flowers are late. In 2026 it was the 36th, from 7 February to 8 March, and in 2025 it ran from 1 February to 9 March. The trees are usually at their best from mid to late February. During the festival they're lit up every evening from 6 to 9 pm, below the Tate bridge near the station and in the cherry tunnel behind the Odoriko Onsen Kaikan.</p>
<h3>Beat the crowds</h3>
<p>Kawazu is one of the most famous cherry spots in Japan, and at weekends the narrow town and the river path are packed. Come on a weekday, ideally early. The trees run about 4 km along the river, so walk upstream, away from the station, and it gets much quieter.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Kawazu Station on the Izukyu Line is 3 minutes from the river. The Odoriko limited express runs direct from Tokyo in about 2 and a half hours. Further south on the same coast, the [[minami-izu-cherry-and-rapeseed-festival]] has the same cherries with rapeseed and far fewer people.</p>
`,

'matsuyama-castle': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from late March to early April. In 2026 the castle had a special light-up for the 120th anniversary of Natsume Soseki's <em>Botchan</em>, from 20 March to 12 April, 6 to 8:50 pm. The keep is lit up every evening of the year too.</p>
<h3>The castle</h3>
<p>About 200 cherry trees, mostly somei-yoshino, grow around the hilltop keep, one of Japan's twelve original castle keeps that survive from before the modern era. It's one of the top 100 cherry spots. The castle park and the summit square are free; the keep costs 520 yen for adults and 160 yen for children.</p>
<h3>Getting up</h3>
<p>Ride the ropeway or the single-seat chairlift up over the trees: a return costs 520 yen for adults and 260 yen for children, and the chairlift is the more fun of the two. The station is a short walk from the Okaido tram stop. You can also walk up in about 20 minutes. Afterwards, take the tram to Dogo Onsen, one of Japan's oldest hot springs. For questions, call the castle office on 089-921-4873.</p>
`,

'yamataka-jindai-zakura': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree usually flowers from late March to early April. Around 80,000 daffodils flower in the temple grounds at the same time, with the snowy Southern Alps behind, so the whole scene is yellow, pink and white. Weekends at peak are very busy, and weekdays are busy too, so come early.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The Yamataka Jindai-zakura is an edo-higan cherry thought to be 1,800 to 2,000 years old, perhaps the oldest cherry in Japan. Its trunk is nearly 12 m round, and its old limbs are propped up on supports. It's one of Japan's three great cherry trees, and in 1922 it was among the first cherries made a national natural monument. It grows in the grounds of Jisso-ji temple, which are open from 8:30 am to 5 pm, with a fee in the cherry season.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>By car, it's about 15 minutes from the Sutama interchange on the Chuo Expressway. Parking is open only in the cherry season, at 500 yen for cars. By train, take a taxi from Hinoharu Station on the JR Chuo Line, about 15 minutes. For questions, call the Hokuto city tourist association on 0551-30-7866.</p>
`,

'funaoka-castle-ruin-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak in early to mid April. The Shibata Cherry Festival runs from late March to mid April, with the trees lit up from sunset to 9 pm.</p>
<h3>Things to know</h3>
<p>The slope car that carries visitors up the castle hill is out of service, and it isn't due back until about April 2027. So for now you'll need to walk up the hill. Check with Shibata Town (0224-55-2123) before you go, because the date may change.</p>
<h3>The view</h3>
<p>About 1,000 cherry trees cover the old castle hill. From the top you look down on the Shiroishi River's [[shiroishi-river-hitome-senbonzakura|Hitome Senbon-zakura]], a line of cherries about 8 km long, with the snowy Zao mountains behind, one of the best cherry views in Tohoku. The Shibata Senoh bridge links the park to the riverbank, so you can walk from the hill straight into the cherry tunnel.</p>
<p>It's about 15 minutes' walk from Funaoka Station on the JR Tohoku Line.</p>
`,

'toneri-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The nemophila are usually at their best from mid to late April. In 2026 the park held its Flower and Light Movement from 10 to 26 April, with the nemophila lit up in the evening, from 6 to 8 pm on weekdays and until 8:30 pm on Fridays and weekends. Entry is free.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 50,000 nemophila fill the area around the fountain and the lawn in section B of the park. It's the easiest place to see a nemophila field inside Tokyo's 23 wards, much smaller than the famous hills at [[hitachi-seaside-park]] but only a short train ride from central Tokyo.</p>
<p>Toneri Park is also a cherry spot, with about 1,000 trees, and it holds a cherry festival a couple of weeks before the nemophila.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Toneri-koen Station on the Nippori-Toneri Liner is right by the park, about 20 minutes from Nippori. For questions, call the park service centre on 03-3857-2308.</p>
`,

'florante-miyazaki': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The spring floral festival runs from mid April to late May. In 2026 it was from 15 April to 24 May, from 9 am to 5 pm. The first half is for the tulips, about 100,000 of some 50 kinds, and then the roses and summer bedding take over. For tulips, go in the first two weeks.</p>
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>Entry is 310 yen for adults and 150 yen for primary and junior high school children, and preschoolers get in free. During events, a special fee may be added on top, so check the website for the festival price.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Florante Miyazaki is a botanical park near the coast, north of central Miyazaki, with themed gardens and a big lawn. It's an easy family half day. Buses run from Miyazaki Station. In late March and early April, the [[saitobaru-burial-mounds]] are an hour's drive north, with cherries and rapeseed among the ancient mounds.</p>
`,

'kuju-mountains': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Miyama-kirishima azaleas usually peak from late May to mid June, opening from the lower slopes upwards. In 2026, the middle slopes of Mount Kuju were at their best around 24 May while the summit was still in bud, and by 7 to 9 June the azaleas on Mount Taisen and Mount Hiji were finishing, while Mount Kuju and its neighbours were covered in pink. So the timing depends on which peak you climb.</p>
<h3>The hike</h3>
<p>The classic view is from Mount Hiji, looking down on the Bogatsuru grasslands with the slopes in pink. Miyama-kirishima is a small wild azalea that grows only in the volcanic mountains of Kyushu, and it covers whole hillsides. This is mountain hiking, with steep, rocky trails and changeable weather, so bring proper boots, rain gear and water, and start early.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car to reach the trailheads, such as Makinoto Pass and Chojabaru. Kuju Flower Park, at the foot of the mountains, is an easier flower stop; see [[kuju-flower-park]].</p>
`,

'lake-onneto': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually turn from late September to mid October, and early October is the safest bet. The forest around the lake is a mix of Sakhalin spruce, rowan, maples and birch, so the red and yellow stand out against dark green.</p>
<h3>The lake</h3>
<p>Onneto is a small lake below the volcanoes Meakandake and Akan-Fuji, and its colour shifts with the weather and the angle, from clear blue to emerald to deep navy. There's a lakeside walking path and a viewing deck. On a still morning, the volcano and the leaves reflect in the water.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>By car, it's about 50 minutes from the Ashoro interchange on the Doto Expressway, with free parking for about 80 cars. Without a car, take an Akan Bus from Kushiro Station to the Akan bus terminal, then a taxi for about 20 minutes. Lake Akan, with its onsen town, is the obvious place to stay. For questions, call the Ashoro tourist association on 0156-25-6131.</p>
`,

'onuma-quasi-national-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually peak from late October to early November, and in 2026 the peak was forecast from 26 October. The park is free.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Onuma is a lake scattered with small wooded islands, below the jagged volcano Komagatake. Walking paths cross the islands on little bridges, and you can see the mountain over the coloured trees. On a windless day the lake reflects it all. A sightseeing boat goes out among the islands, or you can hire a canoe or a bike. For boat times and fares, ask the Onuma tourist information centre on 0138-67-2170.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Onuma-koen Station on the JR Hakodate Line is a short walk from the lake, about 25 minutes from Hakodate by limited express. It's an easy day trip from Hakodate, where [[goryokaku-park]] is a famous cherry spot in spring.</p>
`,

'horomitoge-lavender-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The lavender garden opens for a few weeks in July, usually from early or mid July to late July, depending on the flowers. Check the dates before you go: it's run by a small local business, Yume Kobo Sato, and you can call them on 011-622-5167.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 7,000 to 8,000 lavender plants grow on a slope at Horomi Pass, above the west side of Sapporo, with the whole city spread out below. It's small, but the view of purple rows with a city behind them is unusual. People walk in free; cars pay 500 yen for parking.</p>
<h3>After dark</h3>
<p>The car park is famous in its own right: it's a recognised Night View Heritage site, said to be the only car park in Japan built just for night views. It's open from 1 April to 30 November, and the evening fee is higher. Motorbikes and bicycles aren't allowed in. You'll need a car or a taxi, from Maruyama Koen Station on the Tozai Line.</p>
`,

'hirosaki-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from late April into early May. The Hirosaki Cherry Blossom Festival runs through the season, and in 2026 it was brought forward because the blossom was early, running from 10 April to 5 May. The trees are lit up from sunset to 11 pm.</p>
<p>Don't leave as soon as the trees are past their best. A few days after full bloom, the falling petals cover the outer moat in a pink carpet, the <em>hanaikada</em>, one of the most photographed sights in Japan.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 2,600 cherry trees of some 50 kinds grow around Hirosaki Castle, including some of the oldest somei-yoshino in Japan, kept healthy by Hirosaki's own apple-style pruning. Walking the park is free; the inner citadel and north enclosure charge 320 yen for adults and 100 yen for children in the festival season.</p>
<p>The castle keep was moved off its base in 2015 so the stone walls could be repaired. The work to move it back was planned to start in July 2026 and finish around mid November, so in 2027 the keep should be back on its walls.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from Hirosaki Station in about 15 minutes, or it's about 30 minutes' walk. Hirosaki is about 40 minutes by train from Shin-Aomori on the Tohoku Shinkansen. In the same weeks, [[ashino-park]] has the train through the cherry tunnel, a short trip north.</p>
`,

'kawachi-wisteria-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wisteria usually peaks from late April to mid May. In the wisteria season, entry is by timed ticket only, in two-hour slots from 8 am to 6 pm, bought in advance online or at convenience stores. In 2026 web tickets went on sale on 25 March and convenience store tickets on 1 April. The price changes with the flowers, from 600 yen up to 1,600 yen for adults at peak, so book early and expect to top up on the day.</p>
<h3>Come back in autumn</h3>
<p>The garden reopens for the autumn leaves from mid November to early December, with about 700 maples and a maple tunnel up a steep slope. In autumn there's no timed ticket, entry is 500 yen, and up to two children under high school age get in free with each adult. Wear good shoes for the slope.</p>
<h3>The wisteria</h3>
<p>About 22 kinds of wisteria, a great trellis of long Noda wisteria and two tunnels, 80 m and 110 m long, in purple, pink and white. The tunnels are the famous view, and they're worth the effort of booking.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll need a car or a taxi: it's about 20 minutes from the Otani or Yamaji interchanges on the Kitakyushu Urban Expressway. For questions, call the garden on 093-652-0334.</p>
`,

'maruyama-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Gion weeping cherry usually peaks in late March, a few days before the somei-yoshino. The tree is lit up in the evening during the season: in 2026 the light-up ran from 24 March to 8 April, from 6 to 10 pm.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Maruyama is Kyoto's oldest public park, right behind Yasaka Shrine at the top of Shijo-dori. The Gion weeping cherry in the middle is a 12 m tree grown from the seed of the famous old tree that stood here before it. Several hundred more cherries fill the park, and it's one of the few places in Kyoto where people hold big, noisy hanami picnics, especially in the evening.</p>
<h3>Make a walk of it</h3>
<p>Walk up through Yasaka Shrine from Gion, see the tree, then head north to Chion-in and [[heian-shrine]], or south along Nene-no-Michi to [[kiyomizu-dera-temple]]. The [[gion-shirakawa]] canal, with its cherries over the water, is a 10 minute walk away. It's 10 minutes from Gion-Shijo Station on the Keihan Line.</p>
`,

'bairin-ji-temple-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The plums usually flower from early or mid February to early March. With some 30 kinds, the early ones open first, so mid to late February is the safest time to see most of the garden in flower.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 500 plum trees of some 30 kinds, white, red and weeping, fill the outer garden of Bairin-ji, a Zen temple of the Myoshinji school. A small tea house stands among the trees. The temple itself is a working training monastery, so keep to the garden.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 5 minutes' walk from JR Kurume Station, on the Kyushu Shinkansen, so it's an easy stop. There's no big car park, just a riverside one. The garden is beside the Chikugo River. For questions, call the Kurume tourist information office on 0942-33-4422. In Fukuoka, the famous plums of [[dazaifu-tenmangu-shrine]] are about 30 minutes away.</p>
`,

'mother-farm-october': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The kochia usually start turning red in early October and peak around the middle of the month, often over the long weekend for Sports Day. In 2026 they were at their reddest around 12 to 16 October. After that they fade to gold through late October and into November, which is a softer but still pretty look.</p>
<h3>The farm</h3>
<p>About 8,000 kochia cover a hillside of this big farm park above Tokyo Bay. The farm also has animals, sheep shows and food made from its own dairy, so it's an easy family day out. Entry was 1,800 yen for adults and 900 yen for children aged 4 to primary school in summer 2026; check the autumn price on the website.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from Kimitsu Station on the JR Uchibo Line, and there are highway buses from Tokyo and Yokohama. In spring, the same farm has a hillside of nemophila, see [[mother-farm]].</p>
`,

'tennogawa-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Owari-Tsushima Wisteria Festival runs in the second half of April. In 2026 it was from 15 to 29 April, with the trellis lit up from 6:30 to 9 pm. Entry is free. Parking costs 1,000 yen and fills up, so take the train.</p>
<h3>The wisteria</h3>
<p>The trellis is 275 m long, covering about 5,000 square metres with 12 kinds of wisteria, and it runs beside the park pond, so the flowers are reflected in the water. In the evening light-up, the reflections are the best part.</p>
<p>Tennogawa Park is also the setting for the Owari Tsushima Tenno Festival in July, when boats covered in hundreds of lanterns float on the pond, one of Japan's great summer festivals.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 15 minutes' walk from Tsushima Station on the Meitetsu Tsushima Line, about 30 minutes from Nagoya. For questions, call the Tsushima tourist association on 0567-28-8051.</p>
`,

'meiji-jingu-inner-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The irises usually peak in early to mid June, and they start opening at the end of May. It's rainy season, but irises look their best in soft, wet light, so don't let a grey day put you off.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The inner garden of Meiji Jingu is a quiet, wooded garden that predates the shrine. The iris field was first planted for the Empress Shoken, and it now holds over 150 kinds and 1,500 plants. It's watered from the Kiyomasa well, a spring at the far end of the garden that never runs dry, and which is said to bring good luck. Entry to the inner garden costs 500 yen; the shrine itself is free.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The garden entrance is on the main approach to Meiji Jingu, about 10 minutes' walk from Harajuku Station. Go early, before the crowds reach the shrine. Later in the year, the [[meiji-jingu-chrysanthemum-show]] fills the approach in November.</p>
`,

'sumida-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak in the last days of March, close to Tokyo's full bloom date. There are two festivals: the Sumida Park Cherry Blossom Festival on the Taito side, from mid March to early April, and the Bokutei Cherry Festival on the Sumida side across the river. In the evening, lanterns light the trees until about 9 pm.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The cherries line both banks of the Sumida River near Asakusa, and the river has been a cherry spot since the Edo period, when the shogun Tokugawa Yoshimune planted trees along the embankment. The Taito side has about 510 trees, and the view of the cherries with Tokyo Skytree across the water is the classic photo. It's busy at weekends, so for a picnic spot, arrive early.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>Sensoji temple and Nakamise are 5 minutes away, and a river cruise from Asakusa takes you under the cherries towards Hamarikyu. It's 5 minutes from Asakusa Station on the Ginza, Asakusa and Tobu lines.</p>
`,

'keisei-rose-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The spring roses usually start in early May and peak from mid May to early June. Weekday mornings are best, before the heat and the crowds. Check the garden's website for the spring festival dates and price, or call 047-459-0214.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Keisei Rose Garden is run by a rose grower and breeder, and it's one of the biggest rose gardens in Japan, with about 10,000 bushes of some 1,600 varieties. It has wild species and old roses as well as the latest hybrids, including Keisei's own. The shop sells plants, so it's a favourite with Japanese gardeners.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 15 minutes' walk from Yachiyo-Midorigaoka Station on the Toyo Rapid Railway, which connects with the Tozai Line. In autumn, the roses flower again, see [[keisei-rose-garden-october]].</p>
`,

'biwako-hakodateyama': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The kochia usually turn red from late September to mid October, and the full red lasts only a few weeks. The summit resort is open in the green season from mid July: in 2026 it reopened on 18 July after a renewal and runs until 15 November.</p>
<h3>The flower field</h3>
<p>About 8,000 kochia cover a field near the summit, at about 630 m, with Lake Biwa spread out below. The gondola ride up takes about 8 minutes, and in 2026 a combined gondola and entry ticket cost 2,800 yen for adults and 1,400 yen for children. Pets are allowed for an extra fee. In autumn, migrating Asagimadara butterflies stop off on the mountain.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The gondola base is in Takashima, on the northwest shore of Lake Biwa, about an hour from Kyoto by car. By train, take the JR Kosei Line to Omi-Imazu, then a bus or taxi. For more autumn colour nearby, the [[metasequoia-avenue]] in Makino turns orange in late November.</p>
`,

'rikugien-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak from late November to mid December, among the latest in Tokyo. The garden's autumn night viewing runs from 25 November to 6 December in 2026, from 6 to 8:30 pm with last entry at 7:30. Night tickets cost 1,000 yen in advance or 1,200 yen on the day, and last year you had to buy them ahead, so check the garden's website for when sales open. During the day, entry is 300 yen.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>Rikugien is a daimyo stroll garden laid out in the early 1700s by Yanagisawa Yoshiyasu, a senior official of the shogun, with scenes from famous Japanese poems around its pond. About 560 maples, ginkgo and enkianthus turn around the water. At night the lit trees reflect in the pond, and in 2026 there are food stalls and a matcha set by the storehouse.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's 7 minutes' walk from Komagome Station on the JR Yamanote and Namboku lines. The night viewing gets busy, so arrive early in the evening. In spring, the garden's famous weeping cherry is lit up too. For questions, call the garden service centre on 03-3941-2222.</p>
`,

'mount-takao': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually peak from mid November to early December, starting at the top and working down. The Takao Momiji Festival runs from 24 October to 13 December in 2026, and it's free. From 1 November, the maples along the cable car line are lit up in the evenings, with a night view over the Kanto plain from the top.</p>
<h3>Beat the crowds</h3>
<p>Takao is one of the most climbed mountains in the world, and late November is its busiest season. On weekends the queue for the cable car can be very long. Go on a weekday, arrive by 8 am, or walk up instead: Trail 1, the paved main path, takes about 90 minutes to the top, and Trail 6, along a stream, is quieter.</p>
<h3>On the mountain</h3>
<p>The cable car and the chairlift run up the lower half. The best maples are around the cable car line, by Yakuo-in temple near the top, and on the summit, where on a clear day you can see Mount Fuji. Try the grilled dango and the tororo soba at the foot of the mountain afterwards. Takaosanguchi Station on the Keio Line is about an hour from Shinjuku.</p>
`,

'engakuji-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually turn from mid or late November to early December. The trees near the Senbutsujo hall colour first, and the maples deeper in, around Myoko Pond, follow. There are no light-ups. Entry is 500 yen for adults and 200 yen for children.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>Engaku-ji is one of the five great Zen temples of Kamakura, founded in 1282. Walk up past the big Sanmon gate to the Kojirin hall and Myoko Pond, a garden pond that dates from the founding of the temple, where the maples reflect in the water. The ginkgos along the approach inside the main gate turn gold at the same time.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's a minute from Kita-Kamakura Station on the JR Yokosuka Line, so come early, before the crowds from Tokyo arrive. From here, walk south past Kencho-ji to [[tsurugaoka-hachimangu-ginkgo|Tsurugaoka Hachimangu]], about 30 minutes, for a full autumn day in Kamakura. For questions, call the temple on 0467-22-0478.</p>
`,

'nanzen-ji-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak from mid to late November, though 2026 is forecast to be later than usual in Kyoto. The temple grounds are free and open all day, so you can come early in the morning, before the crowds.</p>
<h3>What to see</h3>
<p>The great Sanmon gate is the centre of it. Climb to its upper floor for the view over the maples to the Kyoto hills; in recent years it's cost 600 yen for adults. The Hojo garden is a separate ticket. Behind the main hall, the brick arches of the Suirokaku, an aqueduct from the 1880s that still carries water from Lake Biwa, are framed by maples.</p>
<p>Tenju-an, a subtemple next to the Sanmon, has one of the best small maple gardens in Kyoto, 500 yen for adults.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's 10 minutes' walk from Keage Station on the Tozai subway line. From Nanzen-ji, walk the Philosopher's Path north towards Ginkaku-ji, or head to [[eikando-temple]], famous for its maples, just to the north.</p>
`,

'yasukuni-shrine-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn gold from late November into early December. The shrine is free and open all day, and the trees look their best in morning light along the approach.</p>
<h3>The approach</h3>
<p>Rows of big ginkgos line the long main approach from Kudanshita to the inner gate, under the giant torii, one of the largest in Japan. When the leaves fall, the broad path turns gold. The shrine is a sensitive place for many people because of its war history, so be respectful in the grounds.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>It's 5 minutes from Kudanshita Station. [[kitanomaru-park-kinmokusei|Kitanomaru Park]] and the [[chidorigafuchi]] moat walk are just across the road, with autumn colour along the palace moat at the same time. In spring, the shrine is home to the trees the Japan Meteorological Agency uses to declare Tokyo's cherry season, see [[yasukuni-shrine]].</p>
`,

'shinjuku-gyoen-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn gold from mid November, and the maples peak from late November into early December, so late November gives you both. Entry has been 500 yen for adults and 250 yen for over-65s and students, and junior high age and under get in free. The garden is usually closed on Mondays, though it opens every day during the busiest seasons, so check the calendar.</p>
<h3>Where to look</h3>
<p>Great old ginkgos stand around the open lawns of the English landscape garden, and the biggest ones, near the Shinjuku gate, are a classic sight. The maples are best by the ponds in the Japanese garden. Early in November, the garden also holds its chrysanthemum show, see [[shinjuku-gyoen-november]].</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The Shinjuku-gyoemmae Station gate is 5 minutes from the Marunouchi Line, and the Shinjuku gate is about 10 minutes from JR Shinjuku Station. Alcohol isn't allowed in the garden. For questions, call the service centre on 03-3350-0151.</p>
`,

'osaka-castle-park-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into early December. In 2026, Osaka's autumn is forecast to be warmer than usual, so the leaves may be a little late. The park is free and open all day.</p>
<h3>Where to look</h3>
<p>Ginkgos ring the castle's moats and stone walls, and the best views put the gold leaves in front of the white and green castle keep. The castle tower is a modern museum inside, and entry costs 1,200 yen for adults.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes' walk from Morinomiya or Osakajokoen stations on the JR Osaka Loop Line. For Osaka's other big ginkgo walk, see [[midosuji-ginkgo-avenue]]. In February, the park's [[osaka-castle-plum-grove|plum grove]] is one of the best in the city.</p>
`,

'hibiya-park-kubikake-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree usually turns gold from late November into early December. It's free and the park is open all day.</p>
<h3>The story</h3>
<p>When Hibiya Park was being built in the early 1900s, this old ginkgo stood in the way of a road widening and was going to be cut down. The park's designer, Honda Seiroku, said he'd stake his neck on moving it and keeping it alive, and he did, which is why it's called the neck-wager ginkgo. It's said to be about 400 years old, and it stands next to the Matsumotoro restaurant, which has been in the park since 1903.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>It's 2 minutes from Hibiya Station. The [[gyoko-dori-ginkgo-avenue]] in Marunouchi turns gold at the same time, about 15 minutes' walk north. In October, the park smells of [[hibiya-park-kinmokusei|fragrant olive]].</p>
`,

'zenpuku-ji-temple-upside-down-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree usually turns gold from late November into early December. It's free.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The Sakasa Icho, the upside-down ginkgo, is the largest ginkgo in Tokyo and a national natural monument. Its branches droop down like roots, which gave it its name. Legend says it grew from a staff that the monk Shinran stuck into the ground here. It stands in the grounds of Zenpuku-ji, said to be the second-oldest temple in Tokyo after Sensoji. Fukuzawa Yukichi, the founder of Keio University, whose face was on the 10,000 yen note, is buried here.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes' walk from Azabu-juban Station on the Namboku and Oedo lines, up a quiet back street of Azabu. Azabu-juban's shopping street makes a good stop for lunch.</p>
`,

'nishi-hongan-ji-water-spouting-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The tree usually turns gold from late November into early December. Nishi Hongan-ji is free to visit, and it opens early in the morning, so you can see the tree before the crowds.</p>
<h3>The tree</h3>
<p>The huge ginkgo in front of the Founder's Hall spreads its heavy branches sideways rather than up, which is why it's also called the upside-down ginkgo. Legend says it sprayed water to protect the hall in the Great Fire of 1788, giving it its other name, the water-spouting ginkgo. It's a Kyoto city natural monument.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>Nishi Hongan-ji is the head temple of the Jodo Shinshu Hongwanji school and a World Heritage site, with two of the largest wooden halls in Kyoto. It's 15 minutes' walk from Kyoto Station, so it's an easy first or last stop on a Kyoto trip. For maples, [[tofuku-ji-temple]] is one stop south of Kyoto Station.</p>
`,

'kumamoto-castle-ginkgo': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into early December. The castle is open from 9 am, and entry to the keep area has been 800 yen for high school age and up and 300 yen for primary and junior high school children.</p>
<h3>After the 2026 earthquake</h3>
<p>A magnitude 7.1 earthquake struck the Kumamoto area on 28 July 2026, and the castle's stone walls collapsed in about 40 places. The castle closed, then reopened on 13 August with the keep and the raised special viewing walkway open again. From the walkway you can see the damaged walls and the repair work, which is a sobering sight after the long rebuild from the 2016 earthquake. Check the castle's website for what's open before you go.</p>
<h3>Ginkgo Castle</h3>
<p>Kumamoto Castle is nicknamed Ginnan-jo, Ginkgo Castle, after the tree that its builder, Kato Kiyomasa, planted in the main bailey. The great ginkgo there today grew back from the roots of that tree after the castle burned in 1877. It's 3 minutes from the Kumamotojo-Shiyakusho-mae tram stop. Visitor numbers fell by about half in the weeks after it reopened, so local businesses are glad of every visitor.</p>
`,

'negoro-ji-temple-momijidani': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak from mid November into early December, one of the later autumn spots in Kansai. The Kishu Negoro-ji Kakuban Festival is held in late November, in time with the leaves. Entry has been 500 yen for adults.</p>
<h3>The valley</h3>
<p>Negoro-ji is the head temple of the Shingi Shingon school, founded by the monk Kakuban in the 12th century, and its Daito pagoda is the largest wooden tahoto pagoda in Japan and a national treasure. The maple valley, the Momijidani, is a walking path through the temple grounds, and it's best seen from the Momijidani Park path. For details, call Iwade City's industry section on 0736-63-5840.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Negoro-ji is in Iwade, in the north of Wakayama Prefecture, close to Kansai Airport. Buses run from JR Iwade Station, or it's easy by car. It's a good last stop before a flight from Kansai.</p>
`,

'tanzan-shrine': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak from mid November to early December. In 2025 the shrine held its autumn leaf festival from 1 October to 7 December and lit up the trees from 14 November to 7 December, from sunset to 8:30 pm. Entry is 600 yen for adults and 300 yen for primary school children.</p>
<h3>The shrine</h3>
<p>Tanzan Shrine sits in the mountains of Tonomine, above Sakurai. It's dedicated to Fujiwara no Kamatari, who plotted the Taika coup of 645 here with the future Emperor Tenji. About 3,000 maples surround the shrine and its thirteen-storey pagoda, rebuilt in 1532, the only wooden one of its kind still standing. The red pagoda among red maples is the photo everyone comes for.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take the community bus from Sakurai Station (JR and Kintetsu) to Tanzan Jinja, about 25 minutes, then walk 5 minutes. Buses are infrequent, so check the return times. For questions, call the shrine on 0744-49-0001.</p>
`,

'metasequoia-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The trees usually turn from late November to early December, a rich russet orange. The avenue is free and open all day. Go early in the morning, before the traffic and the tour buses, when the low sun lights up the trees.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>About 500 metasequoias line a 2.4 km country road near Makino Kogen, in the north of Takashima on Lake Biwa. It's a working road, so please don't stand in the middle of it for photos, and don't park on the verge. Use the free car park at Makino Pickland, which holds about 230 cars. The avenue is lovely in every season: fresh green in spring and snow in winter.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take the Makino Kogen bus from Makino Station on the JR Kosei Line to Makino Pickland. With a car, combine it with [[biwako-hakodateyama]] on the same side of the lake. For questions, call the Biwako Takashima tourist association on 0740-33-7101.</p>
`,

'ishiyama-dera-temple': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak from mid November to early December. In 2025 the temple held its autumn evening opening, Atara Yo Momiji, from 14 to 30 November, from 5:30 to 9 pm, for 800 yen for adults. Daytime entry is 600 yen. Check the temple's website for this year's evening dates.</p>
<h3>The temple</h3>
<p>Ishiyama-dera stands on huge outcrops of wollastonite rock above the Seta River, which gave the temple its name, Stone Mountain Temple. Murasaki Shikibu is said to have begun writing <em>The Tale of Genji</em> here, looking at the moon over the lake, and there's a room in the main hall said to be where she wrote. The maples around the rocks and the old pagoda are lit up in the evening opening.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's 10 minutes' walk from Ishiyamadera Station on the Keihan Ishiyama-Sakamoto Line, or a bus from JR Ishiyama Station. Kyoto is about 20 minutes away by train, so it's an easy half day.</p>
`,

'yoro-valley': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The leaves usually peak from late November into early December, among the last near Tokyo. The Yoro Valley Momiji Festival is held then; in 2025 it ran from 29 November to 7 December, with spots such as the Kengaikyo cliffs and the Kiraku-ri mountain station lit up in the evenings. Check this year's dates with the Otaki tourist association on 0470-80-1146.</p>
<h3>Where to go</h3>
<p>The Awamata Falls are the centrepiece, a 30 m drop and 100 m long, spreading over a wide rock slope, with a walking path below them. Part of the waterfall trail has been closed in recent years, so check what's open. The Kannon bridge in Ichihara and the Tsutsumori Momiji Valley are other good spots. The whole valley is free.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll want a car to see more than one spot. Yoro Valley is in the hills of the Boso Peninsula, about 90 minutes from central Tokyo. The local Kominato Railway and Isumi Railway reach the area, but check for service changes before you go.</p>
`,

'oguni-shrine': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The maples usually peak from late November into early December. In 2025 the shrine's maple festival ran from 22 November to 7 December, with an evening light-up for about two weeks, concerts and a tea ceremony. At weekends it's busiest from 10 am to 2 pm, so come early.</p>
<h3>The shrine</h3>
<p>Oguni Shrine is the ichinomiya, the chief shrine, of old Totomi Province, in the forest of Mori in western Shizuoka. Tokugawa Ieyasu prayed here for victory, and the shrine is known as a place to pray for wishes to come true. About 1,000 maples line the Miya River beside the shrine, and the red leaves over the clear stream are the view to see.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's about 10 minutes by taxi from Ensyu-Mori Station on the Tenryu Hamanako Railway. For this year's festival dates, call the shrine on 0538-89-7302.</p>
`,

'keio-university-hiyoshi-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into early December. The campus is open to visitors and free, and the avenue is right across from the station, so it's easy to fit in.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>A long, straight ginkgo avenue climbs gently from the gate opposite Hiyoshi Station into Keio University's Hiyoshi campus, and when the leaves change it becomes a golden tunnel. It's a working campus, so be quiet and don't block the way for students. The fallen leaves smell strongly of ginkgo nuts, so watch where you step.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Hiyoshi Station on the Tokyu Toyoko Line is a minute away, about 15 minutes from Shibuya or Yokohama. For more ginkgos in Yokohama the same week, see [[nihon-odori-ginkgo-avenue]] and [[yamashita-koen-dori-ginkgo-avenue]].</p>
`,

'yamashita-koen-dori-ginkgo-avenue': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into early December, and they're often at their best in the first days of December. The avenue is free and open all day.</p>
<h3>The avenue</h3>
<p>Yamashita-koen-dori runs along Yokohama's waterfront beside [[yamashita-park]], and its ginkgos hang over the road in a thick yellow canopy. On one side is the harbour, with the old ocean liner Hikawa Maru moored as a museum, and on the other the Hotel New Grand, from 1927, one of the classic hotels of Japan.</p>
<h3>A Yokohama ginkgo walk</h3>
<p>Combine it with [[nihon-odori-ginkgo-avenue]] and [[yokohama-park]] for a flat loop of about 2 km, with Chinatown a few minutes away for lunch. It's 3 minutes from Motomachi-Chukagai Station on the Minatomirai Line. Up the hill, [[minato-no-mieru-oka-park]] looks out over the harbour.</p>
`,

'sobue-ginkgo-villages': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The ginkgos usually turn from late November into early December. The Sobue Ginkgo Festival is held in late November; in 2025 it was the 28th, from 22 to 30 November, with the trees at Sobue Ginnan Park and Yusen-ji temple lit up from 5 to 8 pm. It's free.</p>
<h3>The villages</h3>
<p>Sobue is Japan's biggest ginkgo nut growing area. Around 10,000 ginkgos stand around the farmhouses, shrines and temples, first planted as windbreaks against the cold winter winds off the mountains. In late autumn the whole district turns yellow, and the smell of ginkgo nuts is everywhere. Look for roasted ginkgo nuts and ginkgo sweets at the festival stalls.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Yamazaki Station on the Meitetsu Bisai Line is 5 minutes from the festival area, about 40 minutes from Nagoya. For this year's dates, call the Sobue chamber of commerce on 0587-97-5800.</p>
`,

'sakurayama-park-winter-cherries': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The winter cherries usually flower from mid November into early December, at the same time as the maples, so you see pink blossom and red leaves together. The Sakurayama Festival, to celebrate full bloom, is held on a Sunday in early December; in 2025 it was on 7 December. In 2025 the trees were lit up on Saturdays, Sundays and holidays from 1 November to 7 December, from 4 to 8:30 pm, with last entry at 8. There's no light-up on weekdays.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 7,000 fuyuzakura cover the hillsides, a cherry that flowers lightly in late autumn and again in spring. The mix of cherries and autumn leaves is so unusual that the park is a national Place of Scenic Beauty and Natural Monument. It's free, though parking is charged, and during the light-up a contribution is asked instead.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's in Onishi, part of Fujioka in southwest Gunma, about 40 minutes by car from the Honjo-Kodama interchange on the Kan-Etsu Expressway. By public transport, a bus from Honjo Station takes about 40 minutes to Onishi Post Office, then it's 15 minutes by taxi. Across the valley in Saitama, [[jomine-park-winter-cherries]] has more winter cherries. For questions, call the Onishi office on 0274-52-3111.</p>
`,

'jomine-park-winter-cherries': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The winter cherries flower from late October to early December, with the autumn leaves turning alongside them from mid November. The Winter Cherry Festival is held on the last Sunday of October, from 10 am to 3 pm, with stage events and stalls selling grilled sweetfish and local produce. The trees are lit up during the season, and the park stays open until 8 pm then.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>On Saturdays, Sundays and holidays in the season, the roads around the park are one-way, so follow the signs. Parking inside the park is limited, so arrive early. It's in Kamikawa, in the hills of northwest Saitama, and a car is much the easiest way to get there.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 600 winter cherries grow on the hillside, also called jugatsuzakura, October cherries, because they start flowering in October and flower again in spring. Combine it with [[sakurayama-park-winter-cherries]], across the valley in Gunma, which has thousands more.</p>
`,

'nada-kuroiwa-narcissus-field': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The narcissus field opens from late December to late February, from 9 am to 5 pm. The flowers are usually at their best in January, but it changes from year to year, so check how far they've come before you go. Entry has been 500 yen for adults and 300 yen for primary and junior high school children.</p>
<h3>The field</h3>
<p>About five million wild narcissus cover some seven hectares of steep slope above the sea, one of Japan's three great narcissus spots. The slope faces south across the water to the island of Nushima, and the scent of the flowers carries all the way down. The path is steep, so wear good shoes.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>It's at the southern tip of Awaji Island. Buses run from Maiko Station on the JR Kobe Line, over the Akashi Kaikyo Bridge, to the Suisenkyo stop, but check the timetable, because services are few. A car is easier. In spring, Awaji has much more: see [[awaji-hanasajiki]] and [[awaji-island-national-government-park]].</p>
`,

'echizen-coast-narcissus': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The narcissus flower from late December to February, with early January to February usually the best. The Echizen Narcissus Festival is held along the coast from mid December into January; the 2023 to 2024 festival ran from 16 December to 22 January. Check this year's dates with Echizen town.</p>
<h3>The coast</h3>
<p>Wild Japanese narcissus cover the cliffs above the Sea of Japan along the Echizen coast, with about 60 hectares grown along the shore. It's one of the three great narcissus spots in Japan, and the narcissus is Fukui's prefectural flower. The Echizen-misaki narcissus field is free. Echizen-misaki Suisen Land is open from 9 am to 4 pm in winter and closed on Thursdays.</p>
<p>The Sea of Japan in winter is wild, with big waves and snow showers, and the flowers bending in the wind are part of the scene. Winter is also crab season on this coast, so have Echizen crab for lunch.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take a Keifuku bus from Fukui Station, on the Hokuriku Shinkansen, to Echizen Suisen no Sato Park. A car is easier for exploring the coast.</p>
`,

'tsumekizaki-narcissus': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Shimoda Onsen Narcissus Festival runs from 20 December to 31 January every year, and the 2026 to 2027 festival follows the same dates. The flowers are usually at their best from late December to late January. Entry is free. Parking is charged per day, around 500 to 800 yen depending on the source.</p>
<h3>The cape</h3>
<p>More than three million narcissus cover some 10 hectares of coastal terraces at Cape Tsumeki, looking out over the Pacific to the Izu islands. A path runs from the car park through the flowers to the white lighthouse on the point. Look for the Tawara-iso rocks below the lighthouse, columns of volcanic rock that look like stacked straw bales.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Buses run from Izukyu-Shimoda Station in about 15 minutes. In December and January, the [[izu-shirahama-aloe-fields]] on the other side of Shimoda flower at the same time, so you can see both in a day. For questions, call the Shimoda tourist association on 0558-22-1531.</p>
`,

'edzuki-narcissus-road': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The narcissus festival usually runs from early December to the end of January; the 2025 to 2026 festival ran from 6 or 7 December to 1 February. The flowers are usually at their best in late December and January.</p>
<h3>The road</h3>
<p>The Edzuki narcissus road is a narrow country road through the hills of Kyonan, in southern Boso, lined with tall, scented narcissus. During the festival, farmers sell vegetables and cut narcissus along the road. In February, after the narcissus, the early Yoritomo cherries flower nearby.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>You'll want a car. It's near Hota Station on the JR Uchibo Line, and the Hota Station tourist information office (0470-55-1683) can help with directions. The road is narrow and busy on festival weekends, so go on a weekday if you can.</p>
`,

'kyodo-no-mori-museum-wintersweet': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wintersweet usually flowers from mid December to late January, one of the first flowers of the new year. Then the plum festival follows: in 2026 it ran from 31 January to 8 March, with the plums at their peak from mid to late February. Entry is 300 yen for adults and 150 yen for junior high age and under.</p>
<h3>The museum</h3>
<p>The Kyodo no Mori is an open-air museum in Fuchu, with old houses and buildings from the area moved here and restored, set in parkland. About 130 wintersweet trees flower in the grounds, their waxy yellow flowers with a strong, sweet scent. The plum garden, with about 1,100 trees, is one of the best in western Tokyo.</p>
<h3>Planning a visit</h3>
<p>It's open from 9 am to 5 pm, with last entry at 4, and closed on Mondays, or the next weekday after a holiday, and over New Year. It's in Fuchu, in western Tokyo; buses run from Fuchu Station on the Keio Line. For questions, call 042-368-7921.</p>
`,

'tadao-park-wintersweet': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wintersweet is usually at its best around New Year and keeps flowering through January, one of the earliest flowers of the year in the Tokyo area. It's free.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 80 wintersweet trees grow in the robai garden of Tadao Park in Machida, western Tokyo. Wintersweet has waxy, pale yellow flowers with a sweet scent you'll notice before you see them, and on a sunny January day the garden smells wonderful. It's a quiet local park, not a tourist sight.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Machida has more flower spots through the year: [[yakushi-ike-park]] has lotus in summer, and the [[machida-peony-garden]] is in flower in late April. For a bigger wintersweet display, try [[robai-no-sato]] in Saitama.</p>
`,

'matsuyama-sogo-park-camellia-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The camellias flower from January to early April, with different kinds opening in turn. February and March are usually the fullest. In autumn, the park's sasanqua and winter camellias flower first.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>The camellia is Matsuyama's city flower, and the camellia garden in this hilltop park has about 500 kinds collected together. The park looks out over Matsuyama city and the Seto Inland Sea, and in spring it's a cherry spot too.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The park is in the west of Matsuyama. It's easiest by car or taxi. In late March, the cherries of [[matsuyama-castle]] are in the middle of the city. For questions, call the park office on 089-923-9439.</p>
`,

'nago-central-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from late January into mid February. The Nago Cherry Blossom Festival is held on the last weekend of January every year: in 2026 it was on 31 January and 1 February, so in 2027 it's likely to be on 30 and 31 January. Check with the Nago tourist association before you go.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>Thousands of deep pink kanhizakura, Okinawa's own early cherry, line a 2 km walking path up to the ruins of Nago Castle on the hill above the town. They're bell-shaped and hang down, quite different from the pale cherries of the mainland. From the top there's a view over Nago and the sea. The festival is one of the first cherry festivals in Japan each year, with stalls and local performances.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Okinawa's cherries open from north to south, so the north of the island is the place to be in late January. [[mount-yae-cherry-forest-park]] and [[nakijin-castle-ruins-cherry-blossoms]] on the Motobu Peninsula are both under 30 minutes away, and the [[haneji-paddies-cosmos]] flower in Nago at the same time.</p>
`,

'mount-yae-cherry-forest-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from mid January to early February, the first in Japan each year. The Motobu Yaedake Cherry Festival is held from mid January into early February: in 2026 it was the 48th, from 17 January to 1 February.</p>
<h3>The mountain</h3>
<p>About 7,000 kanhizakura line the road up Mount Yae on the Motobu Peninsula, and in Okinawa the cherries open from the top of the mountain downwards, the colder end first, so if the lower trees are still in bud, drive higher. The deep pink blossom against the green subtropical forest is something you won't see on the mainland. The road is narrow and busy at festival weekends, so drive carefully.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>Motobu is also home to the Churaumi Aquarium and the [[tropical-dream-center]], whose orchid show runs at the end of January. January to March is whale-watching season off Motobu too. For more cherries, see [[nago-central-park]] and [[nakijin-castle-ruins-cherry-blossoms]], both nearby.</p>
`,

'nakijin-castle-ruins-cherry-blossoms': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The cherries usually peak from late January into early February. The Nakijin Gusuku Cherry Festival runs then: in 2026 it was from 31 January to 8 February, open from 8 am to 9 pm, with the castle walls and the cherries lit up from 6:30 pm. Entry to the castle ruins has been 1,000 yen for adults and 500 yen for junior and senior high school students; check the festival price with the Nakijin tourist association on 0980-56-4400.</p>
<h3>The castle</h3>
<p>Nakijin Castle was the seat of the kings of Hokuzan, the northern kingdom of Okinawa, before the island was unified in the 15th century, and it's now part of a World Heritage site. Its long, curving limestone walls follow the shape of the hill. About 630 kanhizakura grow across the site, and the path from the gate up to the main enclosure is lined with them.</p>
<p>The evening light-up, with pink cherries against the pale stone, is the best time to come.</p>
<h3>Nearby</h3>
<p>[[mount-yae-cherry-forest-park]] and [[nago-central-park]] are both close by, and from the top of the castle there are views out to the East China Sea.</p>
`,

'atami-itokawa-cherry-blossoms': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The Atami cherries usually flower from early January into February, the first cherries of the year on Honshu. The Atami Cherry and Itokawa Promenade Festival runs through the season: in 2026 it was the 16th, from 10 January to 8 February, with the trees lit up every evening from 4:30 to 11 pm. At weekends there's free cherry tea, street performers and sword dances. It's free.</p>
<h3>The promenade</h3>
<p>About 58 Atami-zakura line some 300 m of the Itokawa river promenade in the middle of Atami. Atami-zakura is an early, pink variety that opens slowly over a long period, so the trees stay in flower for weeks. The lit trees over the little river are lovely after dark.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>Atami is an onsen town an hour or less from Tokyo by Shinkansen. In late January and February, the [[atami-plum-garden]] has one of the earliest plum festivals in the Kanto area, so you can see cherries and plums on the same day. For questions, call the Atami tourist association on 0557-85-2222.</p>
`,

'tsurugaoka-hachimangu-winter-peonies': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The peony garden opens for the winter peonies on New Year's Day, and they're usually at their best from early January to late February. Entry has been 500 yen, and the garden is open from 9 am to 4 pm. Check the 2027 dates and fee with the shrine on 0467-22-0315.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>The garden lies on the southeast side of the Genji Pond, near the shrine's main approach. About 1,000 plants of some 100 kinds flower under little straw shelters that keep off the frost, a lovely sight in the coldest weeks of the year. It's small, so 30 minutes is plenty.</p>
<h3>Make a day of it</h3>
<p>New Year is the busiest time at Tsurugaoka Hachimangu, with huge crowds for hatsumode, the first shrine visit of the year, so come after the first few days of January. The shrine is 10 minutes from Kamakura Station. In Tokyo, the [[ueno-toshogu-peony-garden]] has winter peonies at the same time.</p>
`,

'hodosan-wintersweet-garden': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The wintersweet is usually at its best from mid or late January to late February. About 3,000 trees of five kinds grow around the summit of Mount Hodo, so something is in flower for most of that time. Weekends in late January are busy.</p>
<h3>The mountain</h3>
<p>Mount Hodo rises to 497 m above the Nagatoro gorge, and the wintersweet gardens surround the summit, with views over the Chichibu mountains. The waxy yellow flowers have a strong, sweet scent, and on a still winter day the whole summit smells of them. Near the summit is the Okumiya, the inner shrine of Hodosan Shrine, and a small zoo.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The Hodosan ropeway runs from near the shrine to the summit in about 5 minutes; a return has been 1,200 yen for adults and 600 yen for children, and there can be queues at weekends. It's about 15 minutes' walk from Nagatoro Station on the Chichibu Railway to the ropeway. For more wintersweet, [[robai-no-sato]] in Gunma is a little over an hour's drive away.</p>
`,

'robai-no-sato': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The garden opens for the season at the end of December, and the trees are usually in full flower in January. The wintersweet festival is held in early January, with taiko drumming, dancing and local vegetables for sale, and entry has been free on festival day. Flowers usually last until about mid February.</p>
<h3>The garden</h3>
<p>About 12,000 wintersweet grow on 3.2 hectares near Mount Myogi, one of the largest wintersweet gardens in Japan. With so many trees in one place, the scent is strong and sweet across the whole hillside, and the jagged peaks of Myogi behind make a striking backdrop.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Public transport is limited, so most people take a taxi from Nishi-Matsuida Station on the JR Shinetsu Line, or drive. Check this year's dates and fee with Annaka City before you go. On the same trip, the Usui Pass and the Megane bridge are nearby.</p>
`,

'azumayama-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The early rapeseed usually flowers from January into February, and the best is often until mid February. The town's Nanohana Watching event runs from mid January to mid February. Go on a clear morning, when Mount Fuji is most likely to be out.</p>
<h3>The view</h3>
<p>About 60,000 rapeseed plants fill the grassy summit of Mount Azuma, just 136 m high, above the town of Ninomiya. With yellow flowers in front, Mount Fuji behind and Sagami Bay below, it's one of the best winter views near Tokyo. It's free.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>The park entrance is about 5 minutes' walk from JR Ninomiya Station on the Tokaido Line, and from there it's about 20 minutes uphill to the summit, including some 300 steps on the town-hall route. Wear comfortable shoes. Ninomiya is about an hour from Tokyo.</p>
`,

'jogashima-park': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The double-flowered narcissus usually peak from mid January to early February. In 2026 they were in full flower on 19 January. A narcissus festival is held on a Sunday in late January; in 2025 it was on 26 January. For this year's date, call the park office on 046-881-6640.</p>
<h3>The park</h3>
<p>About 300,000 double-flowered narcissus grow in the park, with roughly as many again along the 1.5 km narcissus road to the Jogashima lighthouse. Jogashima is a small island at the tip of the Miura Peninsula, linked by a bridge, with cliffs, rock shelves and views to Mount Fuji and the Izu islands on a clear day.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Take a bus from Misakiguchi Station on the Keikyu Line to Hakushu-hi-mae, then walk 5 minutes. Misaki port, on the way, is famous for tuna, so have a maguro lunch. In February, the [[miura-kaigan-kawazu-cherries]] flower along the Keikyu line.</p>
`,

'toshima-island-camellias': `
<h3>When to go</h3>
<p>The camellias flower from December to March, and January to March is the main season. As the flowers fall in February and March, they carpet the forest floor in red.</p>
<h3>The island</h3>
<p>Toshima is a tiny, cone-shaped island south of Izu Oshima, and about 200,000 yabu-tsubaki cover some 80 per cent of it. It's one of Japan's leading producers of camellia oil, pressed from the seeds of these trees, and you can buy it on the island. With a population of only a few hundred, it's a quiet, unusual place to visit.</p>
<h3>Getting there</h3>
<p>Fast ferries from Takeshiba pier in Tokyo take about 2 and a half hours, and the overnight ship about 9 hours. Ferries can't always land in rough winter seas, so allow spare days. There's also a helicopter from Izu Oshima, about 10 minutes, which books up a month ahead. If Toshima is too hard to reach, [[izu-oshima-camellia-garden]] has camellias on a bigger, easier island.</p>
`,

};
